1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,432
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,432 --> 00:00:04,364
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,364 --> 00:00:06,779
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,825 --> 00:00:14,200
PHILLIP ISOLA: Today, we're going

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,400
to have a guest lecture from Jacob Andreas,

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,720
and it's going to be on large language models.

7
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:24,237
So we debated in this course whether to have

8
00:00:24,237 --> 00:00:26,320
a lot of material on large language models or only

9
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:30,680
a little, and we decided to have only a little because LLMs have

10
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:33,240
actually become such a big and important subject that they

11
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,440
deserve their own dedicated courses.

12
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:39,400
So there are additional courses taught by Jacob and Yoon Kim

13
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:41,240
and others which you can take just

14
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:43,880
to learn all about language models.

15
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:47,360
Today, we'll just see some of the basics.

16
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:50,677
But why is this related to deep learning?

17
00:00:50,677 --> 00:00:52,260
There's two ways of thinking about it.

18
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:55,040
One is that, well, large language models are all

19
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:56,420
built on top of deep nets.

20
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:58,460
So LLMs are made out of deep nets.

21
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:00,148
That's the way that it works.

22
00:01:00,148 --> 00:01:01,690
But a lot of people have also started

23
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:05,269
to say, well, what's the next big thing after deep learning?

24
00:01:05,269 --> 00:01:07,677
And one possibility is LLMs really

25
00:01:07,677 --> 00:01:09,510
deserve to be considered the next big thing.

26
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:12,095
It's the next paradigm post deep learning.

27
00:01:12,095 --> 00:01:13,470
So you can look at it either way.

28
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:15,890
It's a paradigm that's built on top of deep learning,

29
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:19,730
but it has new kind of fundamental math

30
00:01:19,730 --> 00:01:23,770
and characteristics that go beyond continuous weight

31
00:01:23,770 --> 00:01:26,850
updates and MLPs and ConvNets and so forth.

32
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:27,350
OK.

33
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:31,490
So Jacob is a professor here in EECS,

34
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:34,650
and he's one of the world experts on large language

35
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:37,050
models, and especially on the scientific understanding

36
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:38,675
of them, not just building them, really

37
00:01:38,675 --> 00:01:39,890
understanding what they do.

38
00:01:39,890 --> 00:01:41,590
So let's welcome Jacob.

39
00:01:44,578 --> 00:01:45,870
JACOB ANDREAS: Thanks, Phillip.

40
00:01:45,870 --> 00:01:46,190
So yeah.

41
00:01:46,190 --> 00:01:48,873
Just to reiterate, first of all, a bunch of the slides that I'm

42
00:01:48,873 --> 00:01:50,290
going to be using today are-- this

43
00:01:50,290 --> 00:01:52,250
is a compressed version of a couple of lectures

44
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:54,052
that we teach in the big NLP class.

45
00:01:54,052 --> 00:01:55,510
If you're interested in this stuff,

46
00:01:55,510 --> 00:01:58,460
I encourage you to take that, which is 6.8610,

47
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:02,000
as well as Yoon's special topics on language models class,

48
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,380
and probably more course development in that direction

49
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:07,180
coming in the next couple of years.

50
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:07,700
OK.

51
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:10,440
This is a lecture about large language models.

52
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:12,790
We were starting to plan out a lecture on large language

53
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:14,540
models, so we asked a large language model

54
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:16,332
to plan a lecture on large language models,

55
00:02:16,332 --> 00:02:18,260
and it gave a reasonable looking outline.

56
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:20,220
And so this is more or less the structure

57
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:21,960
that we're going to follow today.

58
00:02:21,960 --> 00:02:24,460
But really, what this is going to be about

59
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:26,620
is, in fact, mostly--

60
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:29,040
especially after the first chunk of the lecture--

61
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:30,900
abstracting away from some of the details

62
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:33,780
of the underlying architecture that we're

63
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:35,380
using to predict language and just

64
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:37,220
talking about what you can do if you

65
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:42,200
have a really good system for doing language generation,

66
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:44,820
doing next token prediction, and now, you

67
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:47,820
can imagine how to build such a system using some of the stuff

68
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:51,020
that you've been talking about in this class.

69
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:53,980
But I want to start, actually, by talking not about

70
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:57,530
language models based on neural networks, but much,

71
00:02:57,530 --> 00:03:01,510
much earlier generation of tools like this in NLP

72
00:03:01,510 --> 00:03:06,510
and machine learning, which were count-based language models.

73
00:03:06,510 --> 00:03:09,430
Have you talked, I guess, even most basically,

74
00:03:09,430 --> 00:03:12,470
about what a language model is or what I mean by that?

75
00:03:12,470 --> 00:03:13,970
PHILLIP ISOLA: You should define it.

76
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:14,770
JACOB ANDREAS: OK, good.

77
00:03:14,770 --> 00:03:16,350
So in general, the only thing that we

78
00:03:16,350 --> 00:03:18,892
are going to be concerned with, really, for this entire class

79
00:03:18,892 --> 00:03:26,350
today is a model that predicts some next word in a distribution

80
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:29,170
over-- or rather that takes in, I guess,

81
00:03:29,170 --> 00:03:32,230
a sequence of words or tokens or however

82
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:34,510
you want to think of them as input

83
00:03:34,510 --> 00:03:38,175
and places a distribution over possible next words as output.

84
00:03:38,175 --> 00:03:40,050
And if you have the ability to do this right,

85
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:43,390
if you have the ability to see some sequence of input words

86
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:45,888
and get a distribution over words that come next,

87
00:03:45,888 --> 00:03:47,430
via the chain rule, you can turn this

88
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:48,857
into a distribution over strings,

89
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:50,690
you can sample from it to generate language,

90
00:03:50,690 --> 00:03:52,590
and you can do all kinds of other things.

91
00:03:52,590 --> 00:03:55,040
And this basic question of, how do we

92
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:57,863
predict the next word, given a bunch of words that came before?

93
00:03:57,863 --> 00:03:59,280
is something that people have been

94
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:01,400
thinking about in language processing

95
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,080
for a long, long time.

96
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,840
Really, dating all the way back to the end of the Second World

97
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:08,200
War, when people were trying to decode

98
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,300
the outputs of the German Enigma machines,

99
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:12,925
and they had these decipherment procedures that would give them

100
00:04:12,925 --> 00:04:15,100
a bunch of candidate translations,

101
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:17,561
and you needed to figure out which one was most plausible.

102
00:04:17,561 --> 00:04:19,519
And the best way to guess at that was basically

103
00:04:19,519 --> 00:04:22,320
to say, well, how plausible is this particular decoding

104
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:26,240
that I've proposed to produce as a sequence of letters in German

105
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:29,217
or as a sequence of words in German or something like that?

106
00:04:29,217 --> 00:04:30,800
And for a long time, the ways in which

107
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:32,800
people estimated these kinds of models

108
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,540
was just by getting big text corpora and counting, right?

109
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:39,360
So either just saying, well, let's ignore the context

110
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:42,533
and figure out what the probability--

111
00:04:42,533 --> 00:04:44,200
if I give you a random chunk of English,

112
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:46,600
that some word is going to be "the" totally without any

113
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:47,180
context.

114
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:50,120
And if you sample from this, you get a naturalistic distribution

115
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:52,290
over individual words that doesn't cohere

116
00:04:52,290 --> 00:04:54,330
into anything meaningful.

117
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:56,550
If you look at longer and longer contexts, you say,

118
00:04:56,550 --> 00:05:02,295
suppose the last two words that I ever saw were "once upon,"

119
00:05:02,295 --> 00:05:04,670
and I asked you to guess what word is going to come next.

120
00:05:04,670 --> 00:05:09,410
What do you think is likely to happen after "once upon?"

121
00:05:09,410 --> 00:05:09,910
OK.

122
00:05:09,910 --> 00:05:11,485
So some people are saying "time."

123
00:05:11,485 --> 00:05:12,610
Some people are saying "a."

124
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:15,050
Probably both of these things occur

125
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:17,190
with nontrivial probability in a big corpus.

126
00:05:17,190 --> 00:05:19,010
If I just go out onto the internet,

127
00:05:19,010 --> 00:05:22,690
and I count all the number of times that once upon occurs,

128
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:25,490
a lot of those are stories where people are saying, once upon a.

129
00:05:25,490 --> 00:05:27,865
Some of those are typos where people have forgotten the a

130
00:05:27,865 --> 00:05:28,910
and are saying time.

131
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:30,785
There's other stuff that can come next that's

132
00:05:30,785 --> 00:05:32,007
licensed by English grammar.

133
00:05:32,007 --> 00:05:33,590
And if you just count these things up,

134
00:05:33,590 --> 00:05:35,110
you say, every sequence of four words,

135
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:37,485
what's the distribution over things that could come next?

136
00:05:37,485 --> 00:05:40,730
You start to do a reasonably good job of generating text

137
00:05:40,730 --> 00:05:44,370
that looks sort of like natural language.

138
00:05:44,370 --> 00:05:48,310
And this is basically all that the field did from, say,

139
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:52,860
the '60s to the early 2000s.

140
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:55,560
And around the early 2000s, people started to say, well,

141
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:58,660
what happens if instead of just estimating this distribution

142
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:02,220
by counting words in a corpus, I actually approximated it with

143
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:04,900
a neural network model of the kind that you've seen throughout

144
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:05,940
this semester?

145
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:07,440
And it turns out then if you did it,

146
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:10,300
you did only a little bit better than just

147
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:13,277
these big count-based models at this next word prediction task.

148
00:06:13,277 --> 00:06:14,860
So what we're looking at in this table

149
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:16,420
is a measure called perplexity, which

150
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:17,620
you can think of as just a measure

151
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:19,800
of the quality of a language model's predictions.

152
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:22,420
And you get a modest improvement in perplexity

153
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:24,740
by moving from classical count-based models

154
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:27,540
to neural network models.

155
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:31,280
And as our deep learning tool kit got better and better,

156
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:36,060
we were able to drive this number down a little bit more,

157
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:38,660
down to, I guess, the typical perplexity

158
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:41,540
on a corpus of 107 in a corpus.

159
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:43,260
And basically as-- and hopefully,

160
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:45,980
these are models whose names are all familiar to you as a result

161
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:47,570
of being in this course.

162
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:50,050
As our basic machine learning technology got better,

163
00:06:50,050 --> 00:06:53,270
our ability to do next word prediction on a fixed corpus

164
00:06:53,270 --> 00:06:56,190
also got better.

165
00:06:56,190 --> 00:06:59,270
And finally, right now, we live in the age of transformers,

166
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:02,070
and almost all models that we train

167
00:07:02,070 --> 00:07:05,430
to do this next word prediction task in the modern era look

168
00:07:05,430 --> 00:07:06,058
like this.

169
00:07:06,058 --> 00:07:08,350
Now, one thing that I'm playing a little fast and loose

170
00:07:08,350 --> 00:07:10,392
with here and that we'll come back to in a minute

171
00:07:10,392 --> 00:07:13,990
is that I'm just showing you a bunch of numbers or model

172
00:07:13,990 --> 00:07:16,150
architectures with numbers next to them.

173
00:07:16,150 --> 00:07:18,550
But in fact, all of these things--

174
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:20,550
and especially once we talk about the difference

175
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:23,870
between GPT-2 and GPT-3 or some generic transformer language

176
00:07:23,870 --> 00:07:26,310
model in GPT-2, we're talking about changes

177
00:07:26,310 --> 00:07:28,750
in both the underlying neural network

178
00:07:28,750 --> 00:07:30,750
that we're using to do the prediction and also

179
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:32,977
the scale of the data that we are training on.

180
00:07:32,977 --> 00:07:35,310
And so obviously, the other big thing that has happened,

181
00:07:35,310 --> 00:07:37,560
and that you're seeing as these numbers are going down

182
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:41,655
on this chart, is that between 2000 and 2020,

183
00:07:41,655 --> 00:07:43,030
we've developed technologies that

184
00:07:43,030 --> 00:07:45,880
allow us to train just much, much larger versions of all

185
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:48,400
of these models on larger and larger data sets

186
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:50,280
with more and more parameters.

187
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:53,080
And these two things together-- better prediction machinery

188
00:07:53,080 --> 00:07:54,760
and larger data sets-- are really

189
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:59,320
what are contributing to this improvement in prediction.

190
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:03,600
That being said, it is possible, even in this paradigm

191
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:05,160
that we were talking about before,

192
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:06,625
to have a large language model.

193
00:08:06,625 --> 00:08:08,000
So this, I think, is actually one

194
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,375
of probably one of the first references to large language

195
00:08:10,375 --> 00:08:13,880
models in the machine translation literature.

196
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:17,400
It refers to training a model on two trillion tokens

197
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:19,237
with 300 billion parameters.

198
00:08:19,237 --> 00:08:21,320
And you can see that these parameters are actually

199
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:22,960
n-gram counts.

200
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:25,780
What year do we think this paper is from?

201
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:31,440
Yeah.

202
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:32,200
AUDIENCE: 1995.

203
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:33,340
JACOB ANDREAS: 1995.

204
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:37,100
So not quite that bad, but early 2000s.

205
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:41,039
So really, long before these things were in widespread use

206
00:08:41,039 --> 00:08:41,960
for--

207
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:45,410
or in the context of deep learning models,

208
00:08:45,410 --> 00:08:48,610
people were training very, very, very large language models

209
00:08:48,610 --> 00:08:54,490
on large data sets with lots of parameters.

210
00:08:54,490 --> 00:08:57,490
And I think the main point that I'm trying to make here

211
00:08:57,490 --> 00:08:59,250
is that those models were typically not

212
00:08:59,250 --> 00:09:01,790
nearly as good as the neural models that we see today.

213
00:09:01,790 --> 00:09:07,270
So I think there's a habit of talking that people have,

214
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:11,050
especially now, suggesting that, really, more

215
00:09:11,050 --> 00:09:13,592
data and the ability to train larger and larger models

216
00:09:13,592 --> 00:09:16,050
is the only thing that matters and is the only thing that's

217
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:18,433
kind of driving success in AI today.

218
00:09:18,433 --> 00:09:20,850
And in fact, I think you should take this as some evidence

219
00:09:20,850 --> 00:09:22,642
to the contrary, that we have been training

220
00:09:22,642 --> 00:09:26,150
models with as many parameters, as many data points,

221
00:09:26,150 --> 00:09:28,770
as many of the largest language models that we had today,

222
00:09:28,770 --> 00:09:30,550
and they weren't as good as the ones that we have today.

223
00:09:30,550 --> 00:09:33,170
And there really is something special about the inductive bias

224
00:09:33,170 --> 00:09:36,367
that you get from neural sequence models

225
00:09:36,367 --> 00:09:38,950
that we didn't get in previous generations of language models.

226
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:42,700
And we'll see some concrete examples of that momentarily.

227
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:45,540
So just to say this very explicitly,

228
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:47,980
even though we talk about parameter counts

229
00:09:47,980 --> 00:09:50,158
as being fungible today, in fact,

230
00:09:50,158 --> 00:09:51,700
there is a lot of historical evidence

231
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:53,660
that how you use those parameters,

232
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:56,020
specific architectural choices, actually

233
00:09:56,020 --> 00:09:59,820
are quite consequential.

234
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:03,020
So to go, again, very briefly through the history

235
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:07,100
of the field, between, say, 1970 and 2014, a long stretch

236
00:10:07,100 --> 00:10:09,100
of time, people were not typically

237
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:11,620
interested in these kinds of models

238
00:10:11,620 --> 00:10:14,140
as tools in their own right.

239
00:10:14,140 --> 00:10:16,740
Really, it was typical to have some larger pipeline

240
00:10:16,740 --> 00:10:19,020
system, an automated speech recognition

241
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:21,460
system, or an automatic machine translation

242
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:24,940
system, that was generating a bunch of candidate outputs.

243
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:27,460
And what you wanted to do with a language model was just

244
00:10:27,460 --> 00:10:29,340
rerank those outputs, to say, of all

245
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:31,700
of the possible transcriptions of this sentence

246
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:33,660
that I just heard, is it more plausible

247
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:36,500
that a person said they wanted to recognize speech or wreck

248
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:37,740
a nice beach?

249
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:42,620
Or in translation, do all the genders and numbers and things

250
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:44,900
line up in a way that our machine translations

251
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:48,020
systems, which are not that great at the time,

252
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:49,442
weren't all that good at handling?

253
00:10:49,442 --> 00:10:50,900
And so for a long time, people were

254
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:53,220
interested in language models, really, just as measures

255
00:10:53,220 --> 00:10:55,580
of linguistic well-formedness that you

256
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:58,660
could tack onto the end of some other NLP

257
00:10:58,660 --> 00:11:00,900
model that was being custom trained to perform

258
00:11:00,900 --> 00:11:03,220
some specific task.

259
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:07,460
And what really shifted around 2014

260
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:10,020
was that people started to realize that you could just

261
00:11:10,020 --> 00:11:12,300
take these language models and get

262
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:14,600
them to perform the task of interest on their own.

263
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:16,740
So rather than saying, I'm going to train a machine translation

264
00:11:16,740 --> 00:11:18,532
system, I'm going to train something that's

265
00:11:18,532 --> 00:11:20,120
good at recognizing English sentences,

266
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:22,040
and then I'm just going to combine them together,

267
00:11:22,040 --> 00:11:23,457
people said, well, what if we just

268
00:11:23,457 --> 00:11:27,500
pose machine translation the problem of taking

269
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:29,780
a French sentence as input, say, and translating it

270
00:11:29,780 --> 00:11:31,563
into an English sentence as output?

271
00:11:31,563 --> 00:11:32,980
What would happen if we just posed

272
00:11:32,980 --> 00:11:36,120
that as a next word prediction problem of the same kind where

273
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:39,073
maybe these two words are two words in French,

274
00:11:39,073 --> 00:11:40,990
this is the first word of the English sentence

275
00:11:40,990 --> 00:11:42,365
that you've generated, and you're

276
00:11:42,365 --> 00:11:45,110
trying to guess the next word of the English sentence as well?

277
00:11:45,110 --> 00:11:47,750
And so starting around 2014, people

278
00:11:47,750 --> 00:11:49,910
started to take this basic language model machinery

279
00:11:49,910 --> 00:11:54,630
that we'd built up and use it to do sequence generation instead.

280
00:11:54,630 --> 00:11:56,670
And in parallel with that, our sequence models

281
00:11:56,670 --> 00:11:58,390
themselves were getting a lot better.

282
00:11:58,390 --> 00:12:03,230
We invented things like the transformers.

283
00:12:03,230 --> 00:12:06,470
And then what's really changed since 2017

284
00:12:06,470 --> 00:12:10,050
has less to do with the actual models that we're training,

285
00:12:10,050 --> 00:12:12,590
and more to do with the procedures by which we are

286
00:12:12,590 --> 00:12:13,970
actually training these models.

287
00:12:13,970 --> 00:12:19,630
So over here, it was pretty typical to say,

288
00:12:19,630 --> 00:12:21,150
I have my machine translation data

289
00:12:21,150 --> 00:12:23,670
set or my speech recognition data set or whatever.

290
00:12:23,670 --> 00:12:25,450
I'm going to randomly initialize my model.

291
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:27,850
I'm going to train it just on this data set of interest.

292
00:12:27,850 --> 00:12:29,110
And what I'll have out at the end

293
00:12:29,110 --> 00:12:30,610
is a really good machine translation

294
00:12:30,610 --> 00:12:33,150
system and maybe nothing else.

295
00:12:33,150 --> 00:12:36,300
And what people realize starting around 2017-- although, again,

296
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:38,920
this was an idea that had been floating around in the machine

297
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:41,360
learning literature for a long time--

298
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:46,480
was that you could get nontrivial transfer effects

299
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:47,900
between different tasks.

300
00:12:47,900 --> 00:12:51,320
So you could, for example, start by training a model just

301
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:53,540
to do next word prediction on a big data set,

302
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:55,200
and then fine-tune that model to do

303
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:57,120
some specific task of interest, like machine

304
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:00,760
translation or speech recognition or whatever,

305
00:13:00,760 --> 00:13:02,560
and that this was a way to get away

306
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:06,480
with much, much less data for any specific downstream task

307
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:08,200
of interest.

308
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:10,540
And finally, the era that we live in now,

309
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:13,840
which we might call the era of pretraining and prompting,

310
00:13:13,840 --> 00:13:16,280
doesn't even go through any of downstream task data

311
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:18,660
at all, or very little, and basically just says, well,

312
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:21,960
if I have a really, really good, really general purpose

313
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:23,508
next word prediction system, what

314
00:13:23,508 --> 00:13:25,800
are all the things that I can do with it without having

315
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:28,000
to ever even touch the parameters again or make

316
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,720
only very small changes?

317
00:13:29,720 --> 00:13:32,840
And so this is the high level overview of the field,

318
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:35,730
or maybe the history of the field for the last 10 years

319
00:13:35,730 --> 00:13:36,478
or so.

320
00:13:36,478 --> 00:13:38,770
And we're going to talk now in a little bit more detail

321
00:13:38,770 --> 00:13:41,610
about each of the pieces of this,

322
00:13:41,610 --> 00:13:48,130
really focusing on the second two stages here.

323
00:13:48,130 --> 00:13:49,010
OK.

324
00:13:49,010 --> 00:13:52,730
So what does it look like, especially

325
00:13:52,730 --> 00:13:58,010
to operate in this kind of pretrain and transfer mode?

326
00:13:58,010 --> 00:14:01,810
And the thing that people typically did for a long time

327
00:14:01,810 --> 00:14:04,610
was actually not quite this language modeling task

328
00:14:04,610 --> 00:14:07,250
that we've been looking at here, but something that was called

329
00:14:07,250 --> 00:14:08,430
masked language modeling.

330
00:14:08,430 --> 00:14:10,090
And I imagine you've seen something

331
00:14:10,090 --> 00:14:12,850
like this in different flavors for vision tasks,

332
00:14:12,850 --> 00:14:13,795
if not language tasks.

333
00:14:13,795 --> 00:14:15,170
But the basic task is we're going

334
00:14:15,170 --> 00:14:16,610
to take some text as input, we're

335
00:14:16,610 --> 00:14:18,510
going to hide some words from this text,

336
00:14:18,510 --> 00:14:20,927
and we're going to ask a model to predict what the missing

337
00:14:20,927 --> 00:14:23,570
words were in that text, given all of the context that

338
00:14:23,570 --> 00:14:25,030
showed up around it.

339
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:26,530
And what's nice about this objective

340
00:14:26,530 --> 00:14:28,330
is that you can do it without needing

341
00:14:28,330 --> 00:14:30,170
any labeled task-specific data.

342
00:14:30,170 --> 00:14:33,100
You just get all the texts that you can get your hands on.

343
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:35,200
You train a model to perform this task,

344
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:37,500
and you have a model now that's--

345
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:40,980
maybe you can set some of specific classification problems

346
00:14:40,980 --> 00:14:44,072
or whatever up as these mass prediction tasks.

347
00:14:44,072 --> 00:14:45,780
But really, for the most part, this model

348
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:50,352
is not useful for anything in particular.

349
00:14:50,352 --> 00:14:52,060
What it does know-- and for reasons we're

350
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:53,380
going to look at in a minute-- is

351
00:14:53,380 --> 00:14:54,963
a lot about the structure of language,

352
00:14:54,963 --> 00:14:57,180
a lot about the meaning of language in a way

353
00:14:57,180 --> 00:14:58,820
that makes its representations very

354
00:14:58,820 --> 00:15:00,200
useful for downstream tasks.

355
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:05,245
And so typically, what we would do after pretraining on

356
00:15:05,245 --> 00:15:06,620
as much text data as we could get

357
00:15:06,620 --> 00:15:09,780
our hands on with this masked language modeling objective,

358
00:15:09,780 --> 00:15:12,840
we would then -tune this model just using, say,

359
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:15,220
one hidden representation from one last layer

360
00:15:15,220 --> 00:15:17,900
to do whatever downstream thing it was that we were trying

361
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:23,500
to do, whether that's sentiment classification or whatever else.

362
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:25,380
And this was an incredibly effective paradigm

363
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:26,600
when it first came out.

364
00:15:26,600 --> 00:15:28,820
And I think I really want to--

365
00:15:28,820 --> 00:15:32,290
it's easy with the benefit of hindsight

366
00:15:32,290 --> 00:15:35,750
to forget about what a big deal this basic paradigm

367
00:15:35,750 --> 00:15:36,850
was when it happened.

368
00:15:36,850 --> 00:15:42,150
And I think even if you had gone to, say, NLP conferences

369
00:15:42,150 --> 00:15:45,095
for a couple of years before this BERT paper came out,

370
00:15:45,095 --> 00:15:46,470
which was one of the first papers

371
00:15:46,470 --> 00:15:49,030
to really show large scale transfer

372
00:15:49,030 --> 00:15:51,415
effects from pretraining on language modeling,

373
00:15:51,415 --> 00:15:52,790
people would have said, oh, yeah.

374
00:15:52,790 --> 00:15:54,590
This next word prediction task, it

375
00:15:54,590 --> 00:15:57,070
seems in principle like it should be the right pretraining

376
00:15:57,070 --> 00:15:57,570
task.

377
00:15:57,570 --> 00:16:00,510
There was a lot of interest at the time in just learning

378
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:04,630
word embeddings at the very inputs to these systems,

379
00:16:04,630 --> 00:16:06,682
and evidence that you could get good transfer

380
00:16:06,682 --> 00:16:08,390
effects from those, but nobody had really

381
00:16:08,390 --> 00:16:12,808
managed to crack the recipe for getting pretraining to work.

382
00:16:12,808 --> 00:16:15,350
And around the same time, this BERT paper-- and another paper

383
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:17,950
called ELMo, which was actually a little bit earlier than it

384
00:16:17,950 --> 00:16:21,710
and used RNN language models-- showed that you could

385
00:16:21,710 --> 00:16:25,990
train at a very large scale on this masked language modeling

386
00:16:25,990 --> 00:16:27,960
objective and then fine-tune at a very

387
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:31,300
small scale on various other kinds of tasks.

388
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:34,760
These are mostly sentence classification tasks,

389
00:16:34,760 --> 00:16:38,400
lexical entailment, or, I guess, logical entailment,

390
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:42,800
sentiment, various things like that, and do very well.

391
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:44,080
So why does it work?

392
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:46,640
Why is it that we can train a model

393
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:51,793
to do this weird nuisance masked word prediction task

394
00:16:51,793 --> 00:16:53,960
and wind up with the ability to do all of these more

395
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:56,080
complicated NLP things?

396
00:16:56,080 --> 00:16:59,697
And I think the main intuition that now, maybe,

397
00:16:59,697 --> 00:17:01,280
is pretty commonplace, but again, was,

398
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:04,160
I think, surprising to a lot of people at the time,

399
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:07,640
is that doing a really, really good job of even

400
00:17:07,640 --> 00:17:10,760
this simple task of predicting words in context

401
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:13,260
requires both a huge amount of linguistic knowledge-- right?

402
00:17:13,260 --> 00:17:17,119
It requires knowing that what's going to come after was probably

403
00:17:17,119 --> 00:17:19,780
needs to be a past tense verb.

404
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:23,880
It probably needs to agree with the word MIT

405
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:25,140
and so on and so forth.

406
00:17:25,140 --> 00:17:27,813
And you need a lot of knowledge about the world.

407
00:17:27,813 --> 00:17:29,730
If you really want to do a good job of filling

408
00:17:29,730 --> 00:17:31,530
in holes in the sentence, you actually

409
00:17:31,530 --> 00:17:35,030
need to memorize contingent facts about history,

410
00:17:35,030 --> 00:17:38,890
like the fact that MIT was founded in 1861.

411
00:17:38,890 --> 00:17:44,650
And by training on, again, all of the text with all

412
00:17:44,650 --> 00:17:47,170
of the facts expressed in it, we would wind up with models

413
00:17:47,170 --> 00:17:48,890
that actually embodied a lot of that knowledge

414
00:17:48,890 --> 00:17:50,432
in their internal parameters and that

415
00:17:50,432 --> 00:17:54,690
could be accessed very easily via fine-tuning.

416
00:17:54,690 --> 00:17:55,330
OK.

417
00:17:55,330 --> 00:17:59,250
One thing that you do not get out of this masked language

418
00:17:59,250 --> 00:18:01,900
modeling paradigm is a model that

419
00:18:01,900 --> 00:18:03,650
looks like this, a model that can actually

420
00:18:03,650 --> 00:18:05,608
generate the next word at the end of a sentence

421
00:18:05,608 --> 00:18:08,210
given everything that came before.

422
00:18:08,210 --> 00:18:10,330
And the reason for that is that the way

423
00:18:10,330 --> 00:18:12,650
we set up the inputs to these masked language modeling

424
00:18:12,650 --> 00:18:16,290
problems, the model already knows exactly how many words

425
00:18:16,290 --> 00:18:18,210
are going to be in a sentence.

426
00:18:18,210 --> 00:18:20,850
It's been trained in such a way that, often, the missing words

427
00:18:20,850 --> 00:18:22,267
come in the middle of the sentence

428
00:18:22,267 --> 00:18:24,340
rather than at the end of the sentence.

429
00:18:24,340 --> 00:18:26,460
And so while you get good representations, what

430
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:29,500
you didn't get out of any of these bidirectional transformer

431
00:18:29,500 --> 00:18:31,460
models was something that you could actually

432
00:18:31,460 --> 00:18:33,580
use to do text generation.

433
00:18:33,580 --> 00:18:35,580
And so if we wanted to use these representations

434
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:39,020
for doing things like machine translation or summarization

435
00:18:39,020 --> 00:18:42,020
or any other task where there was going to be textual output,

436
00:18:42,020 --> 00:18:43,780
typically, that second stage, the thing

437
00:18:43,780 --> 00:18:46,060
that was doing the text generation itself,

438
00:18:46,060 --> 00:18:50,180
had then to be trained from scratch.

439
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:52,780
So what would it take to go from this kind

440
00:18:52,780 --> 00:18:54,808
of pretraining paradigm-- or, I guess,

441
00:18:54,808 --> 00:18:57,100
this kind of pretraining paradigm, this masked language

442
00:18:57,100 --> 00:18:59,517
modeling paradigm, to something that we could actually use

443
00:18:59,517 --> 00:19:04,860
to do text generation as well?

444
00:19:04,860 --> 00:19:09,020
And I think the main thing that changed originally

445
00:19:09,020 --> 00:19:11,140
was just that people said, OK.

446
00:19:11,140 --> 00:19:13,700
This pretraining thing, this masked language--

447
00:19:13,700 --> 00:19:17,060
this pretraining thing seems very effective.

448
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:20,240
We already have models that can do conditional text generation.

449
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:23,030
We already have models that look like this and that have not

450
00:19:23,030 --> 00:19:24,610
been pretrained in a very large way

451
00:19:24,610 --> 00:19:26,590
and that predict the next word in a sequence.

452
00:19:26,590 --> 00:19:30,100
Let's just figure out which one comes next.

453
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:32,350
Why not just do both of these things at the same time?

454
00:19:32,350 --> 00:19:35,470
Rather than training conditional language models

455
00:19:35,470 --> 00:19:38,350
to output translations or summaries

456
00:19:38,350 --> 00:19:41,470
or whatever, go back, really, to the paradigm that people

457
00:19:41,470 --> 00:19:46,170
were using all the way back in the pipeline days and just say,

458
00:19:46,170 --> 00:19:49,270
give me a really, really good model of all English sentences

459
00:19:49,270 --> 00:19:52,110
or all sentences in all natural languages that's

460
00:19:52,110 --> 00:19:56,670
not specialized to any particular kind of task.

461
00:19:56,670 --> 00:19:59,710
And originally, people did this in basically the same--

462
00:19:59,710 --> 00:20:02,430
or used these models in basically the same way

463
00:20:02,430 --> 00:20:03,770
as they use these BERT models.

464
00:20:03,770 --> 00:20:06,070
You could get representations out of these things

465
00:20:06,070 --> 00:20:08,910
that you could plug into a downstream classifier.

466
00:20:08,910 --> 00:20:11,350
You would, of course, also be able to fine-tune

467
00:20:11,350 --> 00:20:14,110
these models to then perform targeted generation

468
00:20:14,110 --> 00:20:17,390
tasks, again, using translation and summarization

469
00:20:17,390 --> 00:20:19,200
as the canonical examples.

470
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:22,760
And this was really the paradigm that we operated in

471
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:25,680
for a long time, and people just kept doing this

472
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:27,480
with larger and larger and larger

473
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:31,640
models, more data, more parameters.

474
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:32,140
Yeah.

475
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:33,920
I guess these dots are all labeled

476
00:20:33,920 --> 00:20:39,100
with the logos of large industry companies

477
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:42,280
because this was the last year that academic labs were really

478
00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:43,740
playing this game on their own.

479
00:20:43,740 --> 00:20:46,120
And thereafter, most of what we've seen

480
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:49,960
has been various large scale models

481
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:52,540
trained to do just this next word prediction task.

482
00:20:56,160 --> 00:20:57,380
Questions up to this point?

483
00:20:57,380 --> 00:20:59,120
I should say, also, feel free to interrupt or raise

484
00:20:59,120 --> 00:21:00,120
your hands or whatever.

485
00:21:00,120 --> 00:21:02,360
We can keep this interactive.

486
00:21:02,360 --> 00:21:03,440
OK.

487
00:21:03,440 --> 00:21:04,600
Cool.

488
00:21:04,600 --> 00:21:07,723
And so again, for, I would say, most

489
00:21:07,723 --> 00:21:09,640
of the models that we're showing on this slide

490
00:21:09,640 --> 00:21:12,840
right now, the main things that people were doing with them

491
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:14,800
were using them as starting points

492
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:17,890
for downstream fine-tuning or for pulling representations out

493
00:21:17,890 --> 00:21:19,570
of or whatever.

494
00:21:19,570 --> 00:21:22,033
But then something changed.

495
00:21:22,033 --> 00:21:23,450
And what changed is people started

496
00:21:23,450 --> 00:21:24,825
to notice that you could actually

497
00:21:24,825 --> 00:21:28,410
get these models to do some fairly surprising things

498
00:21:28,410 --> 00:21:30,390
without doing any additional fine-tuning,

499
00:21:30,390 --> 00:21:33,850
without really touching their parameters ever again,

500
00:21:33,850 --> 00:21:36,890
once these models got to a certain scale of parameters

501
00:21:36,890 --> 00:21:37,943
and data.

502
00:21:37,943 --> 00:21:40,110
So the examples that we're looking at on the slide--

503
00:21:40,110 --> 00:21:42,490
and this is from a relatively recent paper looking back

504
00:21:42,490 --> 00:21:43,490
at this--

505
00:21:43,490 --> 00:21:47,490
but was that large enough models could start to do arithmetic

506
00:21:47,490 --> 00:21:49,570
without having been trained in a targeted way

507
00:21:49,570 --> 00:21:51,430
to perform arithmetic tasks.

508
00:21:51,430 --> 00:21:53,930
They could start to do things requiring highly

509
00:21:53,930 --> 00:21:55,830
specialized linguistic domain knowledge,

510
00:21:55,830 --> 00:21:58,530
like producing phonetic transcriptions of words,

511
00:21:58,530 --> 00:22:01,330
giving only their natural language input.

512
00:22:01,330 --> 00:22:04,650
They could start doing things multilingual without necessarily

513
00:22:04,650 --> 00:22:07,850
having to go out and collect a targeted data set

514
00:22:07,850 --> 00:22:09,910
for some task in some language of interest.

515
00:22:09,910 --> 00:22:11,410
So the example we're looking at here

516
00:22:11,410 --> 00:22:13,930
is doing a question answering task

517
00:22:13,930 --> 00:22:18,180
where all the questions and all the answers are in Farsi.

518
00:22:18,180 --> 00:22:20,620
And various other kinds of complicated things.

519
00:22:20,620 --> 00:22:25,560
Answering questions about common misconceptions in the world,

520
00:22:25,560 --> 00:22:27,060
answering questions about properties

521
00:22:27,060 --> 00:22:30,020
of physical objects and physical situations,

522
00:22:30,020 --> 00:22:33,380
and so on and so forth.

523
00:22:33,380 --> 00:22:37,020
And this is the GPT-3 paper, which at this point

524
00:22:37,020 --> 00:22:39,340
is pretty famous, but was one of the first ones

525
00:22:39,340 --> 00:22:42,260
to make this observation that you could really

526
00:22:42,260 --> 00:22:50,180
do quite well at a certain scale without needing to do anything

527
00:22:50,180 --> 00:22:51,682
after you had pretrained your model.

528
00:22:51,682 --> 00:22:54,140
And I think this is kind of the defining feature of the era

529
00:22:54,140 --> 00:22:54,848
that we live in--

530
00:22:54,848 --> 00:22:57,182
although there are going to be a bunch of qualifications

531
00:22:57,182 --> 00:22:58,460
that we come to at the end--

532
00:22:58,460 --> 00:23:02,960
that models trained to generate language

533
00:23:02,960 --> 00:23:05,580
start to acquire all kinds of surprising capabilities

534
00:23:05,580 --> 00:23:08,020
at a certain scale without us necessarily

535
00:23:08,020 --> 00:23:12,260
needing to do any targeted fine-tuning for specific tasks.

536
00:23:12,260 --> 00:23:14,590
That being said, part of what happened

537
00:23:14,590 --> 00:23:17,550
here is that people figured out that these capabilities were

538
00:23:17,550 --> 00:23:19,790
present in models, and another part of it

539
00:23:19,790 --> 00:23:21,830
is that people figured out, actually,

540
00:23:21,830 --> 00:23:24,630
more sophisticated ways of interacting with these models

541
00:23:24,630 --> 00:23:26,532
in order to get these capabilities out.

542
00:23:26,532 --> 00:23:28,990
And so the next thing that I really want to talk about here

543
00:23:28,990 --> 00:23:30,330
is exactly that.

544
00:23:30,330 --> 00:23:32,770
Once you have a really good model of the entire internet,

545
00:23:32,770 --> 00:23:34,990
once you have a thing that's really

546
00:23:34,990 --> 00:23:37,030
good at doing this next word prediction task

547
00:23:37,030 --> 00:23:39,710
or doing natural string generation in a really

548
00:23:39,710 --> 00:23:41,710
general way, how do you then take

549
00:23:41,710 --> 00:23:43,870
that model, that distribution over language,

550
00:23:43,870 --> 00:23:47,870
and get it to do the specific task that you want it to do.

551
00:23:47,870 --> 00:23:51,990
And once we start to move away from the fine-tuning

552
00:23:51,990 --> 00:23:56,030
way of doing things, really, the only mechanism

553
00:23:56,030 --> 00:23:59,110
by which we can exert control over a language

554
00:23:59,110 --> 00:24:01,310
model, over a text generation system,

555
00:24:01,310 --> 00:24:04,910
is by changing what we put into it, by changing what words we

556
00:24:04,910 --> 00:24:08,710
ask the model to look at, or the context in which it's making

557
00:24:08,710 --> 00:24:11,520
its next word prediction.

558
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:13,720
And one of the most powerful tools

559
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:18,760
that we have for changing the context in order

560
00:24:18,760 --> 00:24:22,800
to get models to exhibit desired target behaviors nowadays

561
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:25,100
is what's called in-context learning.

562
00:24:25,100 --> 00:24:27,705
And whether we want to actually-- why we call it this

563
00:24:27,705 --> 00:24:29,080
and whether this is the right way

564
00:24:29,080 --> 00:24:30,160
to think of it or not is something

565
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:31,640
we'll come back to in a minute.

566
00:24:31,640 --> 00:24:34,960
But the basic idea here is, rather than going to a model

567
00:24:34,960 --> 00:24:40,960
and saying, like we said before, what's the probability

568
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:46,540
of the word "a" given once "upon"?

569
00:24:49,120 --> 00:24:52,040
If we're trying to do something like a sentiment classification

570
00:24:52,040 --> 00:24:55,160
task, we're going to say, ask the model, what's

571
00:24:55,160 --> 00:25:01,800
the probability of positive given--

572
00:25:01,800 --> 00:25:03,860
well, so let's naively say, suppose

573
00:25:03,860 --> 00:25:05,360
the first thing that we wanted to do

574
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:08,080
is just like-- we're assigning star ratings

575
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:09,630
to movie reviews or something.

576
00:25:09,630 --> 00:25:18,450
So if I said, this movie was terrible,

577
00:25:18,450 --> 00:25:21,170
one thing that we could imagine doing is just asking a language

578
00:25:21,170 --> 00:25:25,050
model, suppose you saw the words "this movie was terrible"

579
00:25:25,050 --> 00:25:26,030
as input.

580
00:25:26,030 --> 00:25:28,690
What's the probability that the next word that comes out

581
00:25:28,690 --> 00:25:32,850
as output is positive?

582
00:25:32,850 --> 00:25:36,690
But if you think about this for a minute,

583
00:25:36,690 --> 00:25:39,650
this is actually a very surprising next word

584
00:25:39,650 --> 00:25:41,890
to come after an input like this.

585
00:25:41,890 --> 00:25:44,065
If you're randomly browsing the internet,

586
00:25:44,065 --> 00:25:46,690
and you come across a web page, and the last words that you see

587
00:25:46,690 --> 00:25:49,250
on the web page were, "this movie was terrible,"

588
00:25:49,250 --> 00:25:53,210
you would assign a pretty low probability to any assessment

589
00:25:53,210 --> 00:25:54,770
of the movie coming next.

590
00:25:54,770 --> 00:25:56,410
This is just not something that--

591
00:25:56,410 --> 00:25:58,670
people don't say, "This movie was terrible positive."

592
00:25:58,670 --> 00:26:00,750
People don't say, "This movie was terrible negative."

593
00:26:00,750 --> 00:26:02,917
And normally, when you're in the middle of a review,

594
00:26:02,917 --> 00:26:06,390
you're not going to get labels for it after the fact.

595
00:26:06,390 --> 00:26:08,300
And so if you just ask your model, again,

596
00:26:08,300 --> 00:26:11,100
as a kind of distribution over natural looking text,

597
00:26:11,100 --> 00:26:13,725
what's going to come next after, "This movie was terrible"?

598
00:26:13,725 --> 00:26:16,100
It's probably going to be putting most of its probability

599
00:26:16,100 --> 00:26:21,660
mass on the word and or the start of another movie review

600
00:26:21,660 --> 00:26:23,180
or something like that.

601
00:26:23,180 --> 00:26:25,760
And so if we want to model, again,

602
00:26:25,760 --> 00:26:30,820
our language model to be a model for this specific labeling task

603
00:26:30,820 --> 00:26:32,780
that we're trying to perform, we need some way

604
00:26:32,780 --> 00:26:34,905
of specifying what kinds of outputs we're expecting

605
00:26:34,905 --> 00:26:37,485
and what kind of tasks we're asking the model to perform.

606
00:26:37,485 --> 00:26:39,360
And so the trick that people have settled on,

607
00:26:39,360 --> 00:26:41,820
which is called in-context learning, or sometimes,

608
00:26:41,820 --> 00:26:46,460
few-shot prompting, is just to create a fictitious document

609
00:26:46,460 --> 00:26:50,020
in which the word positive is actually likely to come next.

610
00:26:50,020 --> 00:26:52,160
And so we'll do that as follows.

611
00:26:52,160 --> 00:26:57,600
We'll say something like, I loved it, positive.

612
00:27:00,140 --> 00:27:00,700
Whatever.

613
00:27:00,700 --> 00:27:06,630
Worst film ever, negative.

614
00:27:09,150 --> 00:27:13,470
And now, if you imagine coming across this page on the internet

615
00:27:13,470 --> 00:27:15,550
somewhere that said, "I loved it, positive.

616
00:27:15,550 --> 00:27:16,830
Worst movie ever, negative.

617
00:27:16,830 --> 00:27:18,410
This movie was terrible."

618
00:27:18,410 --> 00:27:20,950
And I asked you to guess what word was going to come next.

619
00:27:20,950 --> 00:27:22,810
Now, with reasonably high probability,

620
00:27:22,810 --> 00:27:24,977
you should assume that the next word is either going

621
00:27:24,977 --> 00:27:26,110
to be positive or negative.

622
00:27:26,110 --> 00:27:29,310
And given that these labels seem to actually match

623
00:27:29,310 --> 00:27:31,410
the sentiment of the text that comes before them,

624
00:27:31,410 --> 00:27:32,827
you might even guess that positive

625
00:27:32,827 --> 00:27:34,970
is going to be low probability here,

626
00:27:34,970 --> 00:27:38,030
and negative is going to be high probability here.

627
00:27:38,030 --> 00:27:41,470
And so the important thing is that we've

628
00:27:41,470 --> 00:27:45,370
taken the ability to perform generic next token prediction.

629
00:27:45,370 --> 00:27:47,462
I can show you a weird looking document.

630
00:27:47,462 --> 00:27:49,670
You can figure out what the structure of the document

631
00:27:49,670 --> 00:27:52,310
is-- you, as clever human beings in this room--

632
00:27:52,310 --> 00:27:55,670
and guess what word is going to slot into that structure.

633
00:27:55,670 --> 00:27:57,790
And the empirical discovery here is

634
00:27:57,790 --> 00:28:00,910
that once you've trained a large enough language

635
00:28:00,910 --> 00:28:02,810
model on a large enough data set,

636
00:28:02,810 --> 00:28:06,440
this is also something that just generic next token predictors

637
00:28:06,440 --> 00:28:09,080
acquire the ability to do as well.

638
00:28:09,080 --> 00:28:11,380
So in-context learning.

639
00:28:11,380 --> 00:28:13,032
Another thing that you can do here--

640
00:28:13,032 --> 00:28:15,240
and something that we'll talk about a little bit more

641
00:28:15,240 --> 00:28:16,600
at the end--

642
00:28:16,600 --> 00:28:18,880
is what's called not few-shot prompting

643
00:28:18,880 --> 00:28:20,680
but zero-shot prompting, where instead

644
00:28:20,680 --> 00:28:24,400
of giving a model explicit demonstrations for the task

645
00:28:24,400 --> 00:28:26,160
that we're trying to get it to perform,

646
00:28:26,160 --> 00:28:27,880
we just give it a natural language

647
00:28:27,880 --> 00:28:30,920
description of the instructions for that task.

648
00:28:30,920 --> 00:28:35,280
So I guess we have this example on the slide.

649
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:38,980
Instead of an example input and an example output

650
00:28:38,980 --> 00:28:41,420
and another example input, another example output,

651
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:44,040
we just give a natural language description

652
00:28:44,040 --> 00:28:47,100
of the input-- or natural language description of the task

653
00:28:47,100 --> 00:28:50,720
one input and see if an output comes out on its own.

654
00:28:50,720 --> 00:28:52,160
And this, again, was a capability

655
00:28:52,160 --> 00:28:54,400
that was observed to emerge in models once they

656
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:57,960
got to a large enough scale.

657
00:28:57,960 --> 00:29:00,320
And you can imagine doing all kinds of combinations

658
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:01,217
of these things.

659
00:29:01,217 --> 00:29:03,050
You can give both an instruction and a bunch

660
00:29:03,050 --> 00:29:04,385
of few-shot examples.

661
00:29:04,385 --> 00:29:06,510
You can give the examples and then the instruction.

662
00:29:06,510 --> 00:29:09,090
You can give a much more detailed description of the task

663
00:29:09,090 --> 00:29:11,570
that talks about all the edge cases and the format

664
00:29:11,570 --> 00:29:13,270
in which the output should be returned

665
00:29:13,270 --> 00:29:15,905
and whether you want it capitalized or not.

666
00:29:15,905 --> 00:29:17,530
And for the most part, models are quite

667
00:29:17,530 --> 00:29:19,670
robust to all of these things.

668
00:29:23,530 --> 00:29:26,770
We've been through all of this before.

669
00:29:26,770 --> 00:29:29,690
And this works remarkably well.

670
00:29:29,690 --> 00:29:33,538
But as soon as this behavior was observed,

671
00:29:33,538 --> 00:29:35,330
it sparked a lot of debate in the community

672
00:29:35,330 --> 00:29:38,730
about what was actually going on under the hood in these models

673
00:29:38,730 --> 00:29:41,390
and whether we actually-- obviously,

674
00:29:41,390 --> 00:29:44,010
we can draw an analogy between what

675
00:29:44,010 --> 00:29:46,970
we're doing here and training unlabeled data, where I've

676
00:29:46,970 --> 00:29:50,130
given you an x and a y and an x and a y and an x,

677
00:29:50,130 --> 00:29:52,370
and I'm asking you to predict a y.

678
00:29:52,370 --> 00:29:54,090
Should we think of what's actually

679
00:29:54,090 --> 00:29:57,170
going on there as learning the way we think of gradient descent

680
00:29:57,170 --> 00:30:00,110
is learning, or is this something more complicated?

681
00:30:03,050 --> 00:30:08,610
And to piece this apart, there are a bunch of different things

682
00:30:08,610 --> 00:30:12,370
that you can imagine a model learning from an input that's

683
00:30:12,370 --> 00:30:14,770
being presented like this.

684
00:30:14,770 --> 00:30:18,170
One thing that you can imagine it learning or inferring

685
00:30:18,170 --> 00:30:21,290
from this input is just the format of the task

686
00:30:21,290 --> 00:30:23,810
that it's supposed to be performing.

687
00:30:23,810 --> 00:30:29,930
I know from giving you this information that we're

688
00:30:29,930 --> 00:30:31,650
supposed to output labels that are either

689
00:30:31,650 --> 00:30:33,192
the word positive or negative, and we

690
00:30:33,192 --> 00:30:34,710
haven't seen any other labels.

691
00:30:34,710 --> 00:30:38,130
You're supposed to output them immediately after one sentence.

692
00:30:38,130 --> 00:30:39,812
And that you should expect in general,

693
00:30:39,812 --> 00:30:41,770
that the sentences that you're getting as input

694
00:30:41,770 --> 00:30:44,030
are going to be shorter sentences

695
00:30:44,030 --> 00:30:46,243
and maybe seeming to talk about movies.

696
00:30:46,243 --> 00:30:48,410
And so one way to think about what might be going on

697
00:30:48,410 --> 00:30:50,090
in in-context learning is that it's just

698
00:30:50,090 --> 00:30:52,050
about learning the format of the output

699
00:30:52,050 --> 00:30:54,350
that the model is supposed to generate,

700
00:30:54,350 --> 00:30:56,330
and that all the other behavior that you get

701
00:30:56,330 --> 00:31:01,220
comes from pretraining and not from this context at all.

702
00:31:01,220 --> 00:31:01,800
Oh, sorry.

703
00:31:01,800 --> 00:31:04,620
This is just a technical thing that we're

704
00:31:04,620 --> 00:31:05,600
going to skip through.

705
00:31:08,580 --> 00:31:09,080
Sorry.

706
00:31:09,080 --> 00:31:10,520
I'm a little out of order here.

707
00:31:13,900 --> 00:31:14,400
OK.

708
00:31:14,400 --> 00:31:17,140
I guess before-- well, let's actually finish this thought

709
00:31:17,140 --> 00:31:21,680
and then come back to it later.

710
00:31:27,480 --> 00:31:27,980
Sorry.

711
00:31:27,980 --> 00:31:30,080
My slides are a little out of order here.

712
00:31:34,700 --> 00:31:35,398
Yeah.

713
00:31:35,398 --> 00:31:36,440
So this is maybe a point.

714
00:31:36,440 --> 00:31:39,167
So one hypothesis that you might have

715
00:31:39,167 --> 00:31:41,500
about what's going on with this in-context learning task

716
00:31:41,500 --> 00:31:44,460
is just that you're learning these formatting questions

717
00:31:44,460 --> 00:31:45,800
and nothing else.

718
00:31:45,800 --> 00:31:47,780
And if you think about machine translation

719
00:31:47,780 --> 00:31:51,180
as a prototypical example of this,

720
00:31:51,180 --> 00:31:53,940
obviously, you're not going to learn a new language entirely

721
00:31:53,940 --> 00:31:56,932
from scratch from three examples being provided in the input.

722
00:31:56,932 --> 00:31:58,390
You're certainly not going to learn

723
00:31:58,390 --> 00:32:01,338
how to translate from English to French sentences containing

724
00:32:01,338 --> 00:32:03,130
French words that you've never seen before,

725
00:32:03,130 --> 00:32:04,963
English words that you've never seen before.

726
00:32:04,963 --> 00:32:07,950
And so to the extent that we observe these kinds of prompting

727
00:32:07,950 --> 00:32:10,230
behaviors actually making models better

728
00:32:10,230 --> 00:32:12,510
at their downstream tasks, we probably

729
00:32:12,510 --> 00:32:18,350
want to attribute that success mostly to providing information

730
00:32:18,350 --> 00:32:20,990
about the format or inducing the model to better perform

731
00:32:20,990 --> 00:32:23,630
this translation task, rather than all the other things

732
00:32:23,630 --> 00:32:25,390
that we wanted it to do.

733
00:32:25,390 --> 00:32:30,070
And in fact, something that was observed for a little while

734
00:32:30,070 --> 00:32:31,910
was that models didn't really seem

735
00:32:31,910 --> 00:32:35,190
to be paying attention to the associations between labels

736
00:32:35,190 --> 00:32:36,432
and input text at all.

737
00:32:36,432 --> 00:32:38,390
What we're looking at on the slide right now is

738
00:32:38,390 --> 00:32:43,590
an example of a paper from Sewon Min where you actually

739
00:32:43,590 --> 00:32:45,750
took your in-context examples, you totally

740
00:32:45,750 --> 00:32:51,870
shuffled the labels-- so maybe I replace this with positive.

741
00:32:51,870 --> 00:32:54,760
That's not a working piece of chalk.

742
00:32:54,760 --> 00:32:58,200
Where did the chalk go?

743
00:32:58,200 --> 00:32:59,840
I guess it's this side.

744
00:32:59,840 --> 00:33:01,342
Change the label there.

745
00:33:01,342 --> 00:33:02,300
Change the label there.

746
00:33:02,300 --> 00:33:03,920
Shuffle everything around.

747
00:33:03,920 --> 00:33:05,840
These are these red bars.

748
00:33:05,840 --> 00:33:07,960
And for the most part, they didn't actually

749
00:33:07,960 --> 00:33:10,520
hurt performance much at all relative

750
00:33:10,520 --> 00:33:13,680
to giving models actual labeled data for the task.

751
00:33:13,680 --> 00:33:16,015
And so for a while, the conventional wisdom

752
00:33:16,015 --> 00:33:17,640
was that you don't really want to think

753
00:33:17,640 --> 00:33:20,740
of this in-context learning phenomenon as learning at all.

754
00:33:20,740 --> 00:33:23,600
It's really just helping models retrieve tasks

755
00:33:23,600 --> 00:33:27,360
that they had previously learned how to do.

756
00:33:27,360 --> 00:33:30,080
There were also all kinds of other weird pathologies

757
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:31,620
that were observed in these models

758
00:33:31,620 --> 00:33:35,680
where the particular choice of words that you chose

759
00:33:35,680 --> 00:33:38,780
as your labels, if you said positive rather than good,

760
00:33:38,780 --> 00:33:41,220
or the thumbs up emoji or something like that,

761
00:33:41,220 --> 00:33:43,345
could actually have pretty substantial impacts

762
00:33:43,345 --> 00:33:44,720
on the quality of the predictions

763
00:33:44,720 --> 00:33:46,960
that you got from the model.

764
00:33:46,960 --> 00:33:50,160
And also, the models were extremely sensitive to the order

765
00:33:50,160 --> 00:33:52,535
in which these individual examples were presented,

766
00:33:52,535 --> 00:33:54,410
and you could shuffle them around and, again,

767
00:33:54,410 --> 00:33:59,090
get big increases or big drops in performance

768
00:33:59,090 --> 00:34:01,050
out of these models.

769
00:34:01,050 --> 00:34:04,370
However, a lot of these issues seem

770
00:34:04,370 --> 00:34:07,230
to be going away with scale.

771
00:34:07,230 --> 00:34:11,070
And I'm not sure I have slides for this--

772
00:34:11,070 --> 00:34:13,310
no-- in this particular talk.

773
00:34:13,310 --> 00:34:15,929
But even this phenomenon that we were looking

774
00:34:15,929 --> 00:34:20,170
at here, where models seem to be pretty

775
00:34:20,170 --> 00:34:22,770
insensitive to random labels or flipped labels

776
00:34:22,770 --> 00:34:25,713
or whatever, in even larger models than the ones that we

777
00:34:25,713 --> 00:34:28,130
were looking at here in GPT-4 and some of the other things

778
00:34:28,130 --> 00:34:31,130
that have come out since then, models do actually

779
00:34:31,130 --> 00:34:34,489
start to pay attention to the association between labels

780
00:34:34,489 --> 00:34:35,870
and input examples.

781
00:34:35,870 --> 00:34:39,170
And nowadays, for example, if you take a state of the art

782
00:34:39,170 --> 00:34:42,050
language model, and you give it the backwards sentiment

783
00:34:42,050 --> 00:34:46,170
task where this is negative and this is positive,

784
00:34:46,170 --> 00:34:47,810
you can, for most tasks, reasonably

785
00:34:47,810 --> 00:34:49,727
expect that models will be smart enough

786
00:34:49,727 --> 00:34:52,060
to figure out not only that this is actually a sentiment

787
00:34:52,060 --> 00:34:53,940
classification task, but that it's supposed

788
00:34:53,940 --> 00:34:57,460
to say negative for positive.

789
00:34:57,460 --> 00:35:00,180
This is also something that our theoretical understanding of

790
00:35:00,180 --> 00:35:02,900
has improved a lot in the last couple of years.

791
00:35:02,900 --> 00:35:06,860
And we now actually know, in particular cases,

792
00:35:06,860 --> 00:35:09,300
that models are capable of implementing real learning

793
00:35:09,300 --> 00:35:12,200
algorithms internal to their hidden representations.

794
00:35:12,200 --> 00:35:15,500
So if I train a model to perform something

795
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:17,940
that looks like a classification task or a regression task

796
00:35:17,940 --> 00:35:21,380
or whatever, at least on synthetic data in a clean room

797
00:35:21,380 --> 00:35:24,780
setting, there's lots of both empirical and theoretical

798
00:35:24,780 --> 00:35:27,177
evidence now that these models are actually

799
00:35:27,177 --> 00:35:29,260
running something that looks like gradient descent

800
00:35:29,260 --> 00:35:33,300
or some other learning algorithm inside their weights

801
00:35:33,300 --> 00:35:37,020
where the parameter vector of a smaller model being stored

802
00:35:37,020 --> 00:35:38,980
inside the hidden representations,

803
00:35:38,980 --> 00:35:41,220
and that thing is getting updated across layers

804
00:35:41,220 --> 00:35:43,000
as more examples get ingested.

805
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:46,360
Now, whether this is what's going on inside big models,

806
00:35:46,360 --> 00:35:47,120
nobody knows.

807
00:35:47,120 --> 00:35:50,408
Almost certainly, the answer is, it's complicated,

808
00:35:50,408 --> 00:35:52,950
and that there's both something that looks like real learning

809
00:35:52,950 --> 00:35:56,687
and something that looks like task retrieval and all kinds

810
00:35:56,687 --> 00:35:58,270
of other weird, complicated heuristics

811
00:35:58,270 --> 00:35:59,533
that we don't know about.

812
00:35:59,533 --> 00:36:00,950
The main thing that I want to make

813
00:36:00,950 --> 00:36:03,550
is just that this is an area that's moving very quickly.

814
00:36:03,550 --> 00:36:05,630
These capabilities are changing very quickly.

815
00:36:05,630 --> 00:36:08,390
As recently as 2022, I think most people

816
00:36:08,390 --> 00:36:10,728
thought in-context learning was not actually learning.

817
00:36:10,728 --> 00:36:12,270
And now, we think models are actually

818
00:36:12,270 --> 00:36:15,470
capable of doing, at least in some limited way, acquisition

819
00:36:15,470 --> 00:36:20,130
of new skills in context.

820
00:36:20,130 --> 00:36:20,630
OK.

821
00:36:20,630 --> 00:36:22,550
Taking a step back, what does this actually

822
00:36:22,550 --> 00:36:26,750
mean for the ways in which we use models?

823
00:36:26,750 --> 00:36:29,950
And if we remember back to the high level

824
00:36:29,950 --> 00:36:32,230
picture of the history of the field

825
00:36:32,230 --> 00:36:35,270
that we were giving at the beginning,

826
00:36:35,270 --> 00:36:37,510
we've gone from a world where we would go out,

827
00:36:37,510 --> 00:36:39,950
and we would collect targeted data sets

828
00:36:39,950 --> 00:36:42,330
for all of the different tasks that we care about,

829
00:36:42,330 --> 00:36:45,960
and train some task-specific model, to really just having

830
00:36:45,960 --> 00:36:48,200
one model for all of the different tasks

831
00:36:48,200 --> 00:36:50,200
that we care about, and only enough

832
00:36:50,200 --> 00:36:52,840
training data to actually specify

833
00:36:52,840 --> 00:36:54,720
to this big pretrained model what

834
00:36:54,720 --> 00:36:56,220
it is that we're trying to do.

835
00:36:56,220 --> 00:36:57,720
And obviously, the only way in which

836
00:36:57,720 --> 00:36:59,560
we can do this, or the main mechanism, at least,

837
00:36:59,560 --> 00:37:01,352
by which we're going to be able to do this,

838
00:37:01,352 --> 00:37:04,040
is if the data set that we use to train

839
00:37:04,040 --> 00:37:06,960
that initial big model already contains evidence

840
00:37:06,960 --> 00:37:08,760
and information about how to perform

841
00:37:08,760 --> 00:37:10,600
most of these specific downstream things

842
00:37:10,600 --> 00:37:11,840
that we want to do.

843
00:37:11,840 --> 00:37:15,480
And so, in some ways, maybe the most surprising

844
00:37:15,480 --> 00:37:17,800
empirical discovery of the last few years

845
00:37:17,800 --> 00:37:21,240
has just been that if you blindly--

846
00:37:21,240 --> 00:37:26,560
or maybe not totally blindly, but with a limited degree

847
00:37:26,560 --> 00:37:28,880
of sophistication, scrape the internet,

848
00:37:28,880 --> 00:37:31,800
you find examples for translation tasks

849
00:37:31,800 --> 00:37:34,280
and summarization tasks and entity recognition tasks

850
00:37:34,280 --> 00:37:36,560
and sentiment analysis tasks, or at least

851
00:37:36,560 --> 00:37:39,000
tasks that are close enough to them

852
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:41,380
that you learn to acquire all of these capabilities.

853
00:37:45,210 --> 00:37:50,740
And more than that, models get the ability to not,

854
00:37:50,740 --> 00:37:55,430
I should say, just do standard tasks that have names for them

855
00:37:55,430 --> 00:37:59,250
and standard labeling things that have names for them,

856
00:37:59,250 --> 00:38:02,530
but to do more complicated things that actually require

857
00:38:02,530 --> 00:38:07,370
novel step by step reasoning for individual problem instances.

858
00:38:07,370 --> 00:38:10,370
And this is, again, something that both took a certain amount

859
00:38:10,370 --> 00:38:13,530
of skill to discover and a certain amount of actual work

860
00:38:13,530 --> 00:38:15,490
by researchers to figure out how to get

861
00:38:15,490 --> 00:38:17,930
models to solve some of these more complicated reasoning

862
00:38:17,930 --> 00:38:18,670
tasks.

863
00:38:18,670 --> 00:38:21,173
So we're shifting gears a little bit right now.

864
00:38:21,173 --> 00:38:23,590
Up until this point, when we've been talking about a task,

865
00:38:23,590 --> 00:38:25,007
we've been talking about something

866
00:38:25,007 --> 00:38:27,690
like this, some standard thing with a name where you can just

867
00:38:27,690 --> 00:38:30,450
look at a sentence and basically spit the answer out

868
00:38:30,450 --> 00:38:33,090
without having to do too much thinking.

869
00:38:33,090 --> 00:38:35,330
But obviously, there are also lots of things

870
00:38:35,330 --> 00:38:37,793
that we want to do with models that

871
00:38:37,793 --> 00:38:39,210
don't look like this, that require

872
00:38:39,210 --> 00:38:42,980
more complicated reasoning, that take people a couple of seconds

873
00:38:42,980 --> 00:38:45,500
to solve, rather than a couple of milliseconds to solve,

874
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:47,820
or even longer than that.

875
00:38:47,820 --> 00:38:49,440
And so a natural question to ask,

876
00:38:49,440 --> 00:38:52,000
once you've seen these kinds of capabilities,

877
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:54,900
is whether models also acquire the ability

878
00:38:54,900 --> 00:38:58,300
to solve some of these harder reasoning problems.

879
00:38:58,300 --> 00:39:03,360
And if you, again, around 2022, and in a totally naive way,

880
00:39:03,360 --> 00:39:06,760
just asked a state of the art language model

881
00:39:06,760 --> 00:39:08,440
a question like this, for the most part,

882
00:39:08,440 --> 00:39:12,940
the answer would be wrong.

883
00:39:12,940 --> 00:39:14,200
Why is this the case?

884
00:39:14,200 --> 00:39:16,980
And I think one piece of intuition to have here

885
00:39:16,980 --> 00:39:20,020
is that no matter how deep you're

886
00:39:20,020 --> 00:39:25,500
building your transformer or whatever other architecture

887
00:39:25,500 --> 00:39:29,340
you're using for your language model,

888
00:39:29,340 --> 00:39:32,540
you only have a finite amount of computational resources

889
00:39:32,540 --> 00:39:34,820
available to you between the moment

890
00:39:34,820 --> 00:39:37,660
when you read the last word in the input and the moment

891
00:39:37,660 --> 00:39:39,870
at which you're expected to generate

892
00:39:39,870 --> 00:39:41,730
the first word in the output.

893
00:39:41,730 --> 00:39:44,130
And so if you think about a task like this,

894
00:39:44,130 --> 00:39:47,750
really, the model has to see the question mark token or the word

895
00:39:47,750 --> 00:39:50,330
have or whatever at the end of this question

896
00:39:50,330 --> 00:39:51,830
and do all of the work that it needs

897
00:39:51,830 --> 00:39:54,790
to solve this hard arithmetic problem somewhere internal

898
00:39:54,790 --> 00:39:57,110
to its intermediate states.

899
00:39:57,110 --> 00:40:00,005
And not to anthropomorphize these things too much,

900
00:40:00,005 --> 00:40:01,630
but think about this as trying to solve

901
00:40:01,630 --> 00:40:04,310
a problem like this in your head with a very, very

902
00:40:04,310 --> 00:40:06,150
small amount of time.

903
00:40:06,150 --> 00:40:09,670
And basically, I think we should expect-- or at least not be

904
00:40:09,670 --> 00:40:13,310
surprised-- that models are not able to do this out of the box.

905
00:40:13,310 --> 00:40:17,430
So what do people do when they solve problems like this

906
00:40:17,430 --> 00:40:20,070
if it's not just generating the answer right away?

907
00:40:20,070 --> 00:40:22,402
Well, in some amount of step by step thinking, right?

908
00:40:22,402 --> 00:40:24,110
Either writing out the intermediate steps

909
00:40:24,110 --> 00:40:26,550
of an algorithm on some of scratchpad,

910
00:40:26,550 --> 00:40:30,150
talking out loud through a solution, or so on and so forth.

911
00:40:30,150 --> 00:40:33,070
And maybe unsurprisingly, something

912
00:40:33,070 --> 00:40:37,190
that has been observed, again, once you get to language models

913
00:40:37,190 --> 00:40:39,000
at a sufficiently large scale, is

914
00:40:39,000 --> 00:40:41,080
that this kind of step by step reasoning

915
00:40:41,080 --> 00:40:43,000
works for them as well.

916
00:40:43,000 --> 00:40:45,560
And so this has come to be called chain-of-thought

917
00:40:45,560 --> 00:40:48,280
prompting-- it's something that I think the LLM hobbyist

918
00:40:48,280 --> 00:40:53,200
community was aware of before it entered the academic discourse--

919
00:40:53,200 --> 00:40:58,320
but where we now give models inputs

920
00:40:58,320 --> 00:41:01,040
that force them to reason more sophisticatedly, again, by just

921
00:41:01,040 --> 00:41:02,340
changing the inputs to them.

922
00:41:02,340 --> 00:41:05,945
So just like in the few-shot prompting case,

923
00:41:05,945 --> 00:41:08,320
we're interested in improving the capabilities of models,

924
00:41:08,320 --> 00:41:11,920
and the only mechanism by which we can control their behavior

925
00:41:11,920 --> 00:41:14,040
is to change the inputs that we give them.

926
00:41:14,040 --> 00:41:16,925
And so now, when we're giving demonstrations of a task,

927
00:41:16,925 --> 00:41:18,800
rather than just saying, here's the question,

928
00:41:18,800 --> 00:41:20,820
and here's the answer, we're going to say,

929
00:41:20,820 --> 00:41:22,492
here's the question.

930
00:41:22,492 --> 00:41:24,200
Here's the bit of the answer that we want

931
00:41:24,200 --> 00:41:25,500
the language model to generate.

932
00:41:25,500 --> 00:41:26,960
And the first piece of that answer

933
00:41:26,960 --> 00:41:30,805
should include not the final output to this question,

934
00:41:30,805 --> 00:41:32,680
but this reasoning steps that will eventually

935
00:41:32,680 --> 00:41:35,040
lead us to the right answer.

936
00:41:35,040 --> 00:41:37,170
And so going back to the example that we

937
00:41:37,170 --> 00:41:40,690
had on the previous slide, rather than just saying, here's

938
00:41:40,690 --> 00:41:44,650
the question, and 11 comes out, we say, here's the question.

939
00:41:44,650 --> 00:41:46,070
Roger starts with 5 balls.

940
00:41:46,070 --> 00:41:48,570
2 cans of 3 tennis balls each is 6 balls.

941
00:41:48,570 --> 00:41:49,900
5 plus 6 is 11.

942
00:41:49,900 --> 00:41:51,650
The kinds of things that you might imagine

943
00:41:51,650 --> 00:41:54,930
saying out loud to yourself or writing down in the course

944
00:41:54,930 --> 00:41:56,350
of doing a problem like this.

945
00:41:56,350 --> 00:41:58,790
And when you do this, when you prompt models in this way,

946
00:41:58,790 --> 00:42:00,755
and then you give them new questions,

947
00:42:00,755 --> 00:42:02,630
they're pattern matching against the context.

948
00:42:02,630 --> 00:42:05,970
And just like we saw in these few-shot prompting examples,

949
00:42:05,970 --> 00:42:09,010
what you get is an answer that first includes a bunch of step

950
00:42:09,010 --> 00:42:11,690
by step reasoning, maybe following a different pattern

951
00:42:11,690 --> 00:42:15,130
from the one that we provided in the input, and then the answer

952
00:42:15,130 --> 00:42:17,250
at the end.

953
00:42:17,250 --> 00:42:19,210
And obviously, this is something that you

954
00:42:19,210 --> 00:42:21,210
can combine with all of the other tricks

955
00:42:21,210 --> 00:42:24,410
that we were talking about in the context of few-shot

956
00:42:24,410 --> 00:42:29,050
prompting, meaning you can also provide high level

957
00:42:29,050 --> 00:42:30,350
description of the task.

958
00:42:30,350 --> 00:42:32,670
You can also provide a meta template,

959
00:42:32,670 --> 00:42:35,193
the kinds of things that make good step by step reasoning

960
00:42:35,193 --> 00:42:37,860
instructions, information about the granularity of the reasoning

961
00:42:37,860 --> 00:42:40,445
steps and so on, information about the format

962
00:42:40,445 --> 00:42:42,820
in which the final answer should be presented so that you

963
00:42:42,820 --> 00:42:45,980
can parse it out, and so on.

964
00:42:45,980 --> 00:42:48,740
But really, the high level idea here is just rather--

965
00:42:48,740 --> 00:42:50,900
between our x's and our y's, we're

966
00:42:50,900 --> 00:42:56,072
going to interpose some reasoning steps.

967
00:42:56,072 --> 00:42:57,780
And if you do this in the input, then you

968
00:42:57,780 --> 00:43:02,100
expect to get it in the output as well.

969
00:43:02,100 --> 00:43:04,080
And so this is just writing this more formally.

970
00:43:04,080 --> 00:43:07,380
We're going to, between our x's and our y's, put

971
00:43:07,380 --> 00:43:11,340
some z's that show the model how to reason.

972
00:43:11,340 --> 00:43:13,860
And because everything's in the input

973
00:43:13,860 --> 00:43:15,700
because we're in this very few-shot paradigm

974
00:43:15,700 --> 00:43:18,140
that we were looking at over here,

975
00:43:18,140 --> 00:43:19,580
it's really not that hard for you

976
00:43:19,580 --> 00:43:21,300
as an experimenter or a model builder

977
00:43:21,300 --> 00:43:24,900
or whatever to write 5 or 10 or 100 examples of these things

978
00:43:24,900 --> 00:43:28,860
to provide as input to the model.

979
00:43:28,860 --> 00:43:31,830
So this works in a bunch of different regimes.

980
00:43:31,830 --> 00:43:34,470
Again, I think something that maybe is worth

981
00:43:34,470 --> 00:43:37,990
emphasizing is that this is a capability that arose,

982
00:43:37,990 --> 00:43:41,910
just from a historical perspective, a little bit

983
00:43:41,910 --> 00:43:44,730
unpredictably and only at a certain scale.

984
00:43:44,730 --> 00:43:48,070
So I knew of at least three different groups at MIT

985
00:43:48,070 --> 00:43:50,382
around 2019 who were trying to do something like this,

986
00:43:50,382 --> 00:43:52,590
and it wasn't working, because the models that we had

987
00:43:52,590 --> 00:43:54,230
at the time weren't big enough.

988
00:43:54,230 --> 00:43:57,170
And then GPT-3 came out, and people tried it again,

989
00:43:57,170 --> 00:43:59,390
and all these things work.

990
00:43:59,390 --> 00:44:02,010
You can do this for free response math questions.

991
00:44:02,010 --> 00:44:03,930
You can use it for multiple choice questions.

992
00:44:03,930 --> 00:44:06,190
You can do it for common sense reasoning.

993
00:44:06,190 --> 00:44:09,110
And really, this is a meta trick that you

994
00:44:09,110 --> 00:44:13,510
can apply to almost any domain of interest.

995
00:44:13,510 --> 00:44:17,410
So here are some more examples of places you can do this.

996
00:44:17,410 --> 00:44:18,790
It's really quite general.

997
00:44:18,790 --> 00:44:20,810
And it's really, really, really quite effective,

998
00:44:20,810 --> 00:44:23,750
especially on these problems that require a lot of step

999
00:44:23,750 --> 00:44:24,590
by step reasoning.

1000
00:44:24,590 --> 00:44:25,210
Questions?

1001
00:44:25,210 --> 00:44:26,190
Yes.

1002
00:44:26,190 --> 00:44:28,817
AUDIENCE: Why do you think scale made it possible?

1003
00:44:28,817 --> 00:44:30,900
JACOB ANDREAS: So why does scale make it possible?

1004
00:44:30,900 --> 00:44:32,742
I mean, again, I think even the-- well,

1005
00:44:32,742 --> 00:44:34,700
so there's a couple of different things, right?

1006
00:44:34,700 --> 00:44:43,120
There's recognizing that a given input is asking for a model

1007
00:44:43,120 --> 00:44:45,880
to perform some of abstract reasoning capability and not

1008
00:44:45,880 --> 00:44:48,980
just to pattern match against a template.

1009
00:44:48,980 --> 00:44:50,600
And so I think a meaningful difference

1010
00:44:50,600 --> 00:44:54,840
between some of these examples and some of the things

1011
00:44:54,840 --> 00:44:56,500
that we were looking at over here

1012
00:44:56,500 --> 00:44:59,125
is that what you put into that z in principle

1013
00:44:59,125 --> 00:45:01,000
looks very different from problem to problem.

1014
00:45:01,000 --> 00:45:03,440
You're not just filling in holes in a template.

1015
00:45:03,440 --> 00:45:05,533
And that's something that-- small models,

1016
00:45:05,533 --> 00:45:06,700
you can give them this data.

1017
00:45:06,700 --> 00:45:09,120
They'll do the copying bit, and they won't do the generalization

1018
00:45:09,120 --> 00:45:09,580
bit.

1019
00:45:09,580 --> 00:45:11,205
And it's like the ability to generalize

1020
00:45:11,205 --> 00:45:14,297
to new reasoning chains, that's the thing that you get.

1021
00:45:14,297 --> 00:45:15,880
I think it is also the case-- and this

1022
00:45:15,880 --> 00:45:17,713
is something we'll come back to at the end--

1023
00:45:17,713 --> 00:45:20,040
that in the very largest models where people apply

1024
00:45:20,040 --> 00:45:23,400
these things now, there is some large amount of deliberate data

1025
00:45:23,400 --> 00:45:26,200
collection where people are making sure in the training set

1026
00:45:26,200 --> 00:45:28,970
there are enough examples of these kinds of step

1027
00:45:28,970 --> 00:45:31,202
by step thinking out loud.

1028
00:45:31,202 --> 00:45:31,910
Another question.

1029
00:45:31,910 --> 00:45:32,770
Yeah.

1030
00:45:32,770 --> 00:45:36,290
AUDIENCE: It seems like you can enforce multi-step reasoning

1031
00:45:36,290 --> 00:45:37,835
through chain-of-thought prompting.

1032
00:45:37,835 --> 00:45:40,210
And I'm wondering, where does reinforcement learning fall

1033
00:45:40,210 --> 00:45:40,670
into that?

1034
00:45:40,670 --> 00:45:42,430
Is that just to make it marginally better?

1035
00:45:42,430 --> 00:45:43,370
JACOB ANDREAS: So there's a question

1036
00:45:43,370 --> 00:45:44,670
about reinforcement learning.

1037
00:45:44,670 --> 00:45:49,970
We'll talk about reinforcement learning at the end.

1038
00:45:49,970 --> 00:45:51,970
Cool.

1039
00:45:51,970 --> 00:45:52,510
Good.

1040
00:45:52,510 --> 00:45:54,385
And I think maybe the main thing to take away

1041
00:45:54,385 --> 00:45:58,080
from this is just that you can, really,

1042
00:45:58,080 --> 00:45:59,830
for some of these hard reasoning problems,

1043
00:45:59,830 --> 00:46:02,433
especially in larger models, dramatically

1044
00:46:02,433 --> 00:46:03,850
improve the quality of the outputs

1045
00:46:03,850 --> 00:46:08,130
that you get just by prompting models to think step by step

1046
00:46:08,130 --> 00:46:10,170
in their outputs, by providing examples

1047
00:46:10,170 --> 00:46:12,130
of the kinds of reasoning chains that you

1048
00:46:12,130 --> 00:46:14,850
want them to go through.

1049
00:46:14,850 --> 00:46:19,610
Even more infuriatingly, as soon as the first round

1050
00:46:19,610 --> 00:46:22,730
of -of-thought papers came out where people were manually

1051
00:46:22,730 --> 00:46:27,300
authoring these z's, these reasoning steps,

1052
00:46:27,300 --> 00:46:30,180
people started to ask, is there just some magic incantation

1053
00:46:30,180 --> 00:46:31,900
that you can give models so that I

1054
00:46:31,900 --> 00:46:34,523
don't have to demonstrate the reasoning steps anew

1055
00:46:34,523 --> 00:46:36,440
for every new task that I'm trying to perform,

1056
00:46:36,440 --> 00:46:39,420
but I can just prompt the model in a totally task generic way

1057
00:46:39,420 --> 00:46:41,580
that will cause it to do this reasoning

1058
00:46:41,580 --> 00:46:43,280
and then produce a final answer?

1059
00:46:47,620 --> 00:46:49,460
So as you might expect, somebody basically

1060
00:46:49,460 --> 00:46:54,412
just did a brute force search in the space of magic incantations

1061
00:46:54,412 --> 00:46:56,620
that you could append to the beginning of your answer

1062
00:46:56,620 --> 00:46:58,460
in a language model.

1063
00:46:58,460 --> 00:47:00,300
They found, "Let's think step by step,"

1064
00:47:00,300 --> 00:47:02,500
is the best string for, at least,

1065
00:47:02,500 --> 00:47:05,580
the models that existed in 2022.

1066
00:47:05,580 --> 00:47:13,580
And again, just maddeningly, this works super well.

1067
00:47:13,580 --> 00:47:17,140
The way to read this chart is you can take a model that gets

1068
00:47:17,140 --> 00:47:20,460
18% on an arithmetic task and bring it up to 80%

1069
00:47:20,460 --> 00:47:22,640
on an arithmetic task just by typing,

1070
00:47:22,640 --> 00:47:25,190
"Let's think step by step," before you ask the model

1071
00:47:25,190 --> 00:47:27,470
to generate an answer.

1072
00:47:27,470 --> 00:47:33,950
Again, whether this really reflects some deep or unexpected

1073
00:47:33,950 --> 00:47:35,550
property of the training distribution,

1074
00:47:35,550 --> 00:47:39,950
or whether somewhere on the internet or somewhere deep

1075
00:47:39,950 --> 00:47:42,650
inside OpenAI, some product manager said, hey,

1076
00:47:42,650 --> 00:47:44,927
let's go out and generate a lot of documents that say,

1077
00:47:44,927 --> 00:47:47,510
"Let's think step by step," and then have the reasoning steps,

1078
00:47:47,510 --> 00:47:48,210
we don't know.

1079
00:47:48,210 --> 00:47:49,510
We may never know.

1080
00:47:49,510 --> 00:47:51,350
What we do know is that there are actually

1081
00:47:51,350 --> 00:47:54,788
these just kind of magic phrases that you

1082
00:47:54,788 --> 00:47:56,830
can give to models that cause them to become much

1083
00:47:56,830 --> 00:47:58,290
better at some of these tasks.

1084
00:48:02,750 --> 00:48:03,430
Good.

1085
00:48:03,430 --> 00:48:06,270
And obviously, this is--

1086
00:48:06,270 --> 00:48:08,630
even though I've been calling it magic, it is not magic.

1087
00:48:08,630 --> 00:48:10,870
And in general, you are always going

1088
00:48:10,870 --> 00:48:14,550
to do better by giving explicit examples of the kind

1089
00:48:14,550 --> 00:48:16,130
of reasoning that you want to see,

1090
00:48:16,130 --> 00:48:19,130
rather than asking models to come up with it on their own.

1091
00:48:19,130 --> 00:48:22,820
But they are sometimes, to a nontrivial degree,

1092
00:48:22,820 --> 00:48:24,912
able to come up with it on their own.

1093
00:48:24,912 --> 00:48:27,120
Here are a bunch of different prompts that were tried

1094
00:48:27,120 --> 00:48:30,400
and varying degrees of effectiveness.

1095
00:48:33,320 --> 00:48:34,640
Cool.

1096
00:48:34,640 --> 00:48:36,502
Here is a meme that we found when

1097
00:48:36,502 --> 00:48:37,960
we were putting this slide together

1098
00:48:37,960 --> 00:48:42,280
that, back when I was starting grad school,

1099
00:48:42,280 --> 00:48:45,280
all kinds of beautiful math and structured prediction things,

1100
00:48:45,280 --> 00:48:47,183
and now, really, the most effective thing

1101
00:48:47,183 --> 00:48:48,600
that you can do for a lot of tasks

1102
00:48:48,600 --> 00:48:50,440
is to write the words step by step.

1103
00:48:50,440 --> 00:48:54,760
And this is some amount of NLP research, but not all of it.

1104
00:48:54,760 --> 00:48:56,880
And I think there's a higher level

1105
00:48:56,880 --> 00:49:01,200
thing here, which is that a weird feature of living

1106
00:49:01,200 --> 00:49:04,560
in the modern era is that, actually, one of the greatest

1107
00:49:04,560 --> 00:49:07,600
degrees of control we have over models, one of the best

1108
00:49:07,600 --> 00:49:10,160
tools that we have for making them better,

1109
00:49:10,160 --> 00:49:14,340
is just coming up with better examples for the input,

1110
00:49:14,340 --> 00:49:17,948
coming up with better formats in which models can generate

1111
00:49:17,948 --> 00:49:20,240
their output, better guidance for what kinds of outputs

1112
00:49:20,240 --> 00:49:22,940
they should provide without doing any kind of fine-tuning.

1113
00:49:22,940 --> 00:49:25,680
And so whether or not you think this is interesting research

1114
00:49:25,680 --> 00:49:27,055
at this point, certainly, there's

1115
00:49:27,055 --> 00:49:29,640
an enormous amount of demand in industry

1116
00:49:29,640 --> 00:49:32,560
for people who have just very good intuition for how

1117
00:49:32,560 --> 00:49:36,440
to come up with strings that look like this

1118
00:49:36,440 --> 00:49:38,440
and other ways of structuring models and getting

1119
00:49:38,440 --> 00:49:41,680
their outputs to come.

1120
00:49:41,680 --> 00:49:42,980
There are things to learn.

1121
00:49:42,980 --> 00:49:45,240
My students are much better at this than I am.

1122
00:49:45,240 --> 00:49:50,360
And it's a thing that people get better at over time.

1123
00:49:50,360 --> 00:49:57,280
But how do we push this beyond what you get from off the shelf

1124
00:49:57,280 --> 00:50:00,860
models that have just been trained on the whole internet?

1125
00:50:00,860 --> 00:50:02,360
And in particular, is there anything

1126
00:50:02,360 --> 00:50:05,578
that we can do during the pretraining phase, knowing

1127
00:50:05,578 --> 00:50:08,120
that the ways in which these models are going to get used now

1128
00:50:08,120 --> 00:50:12,120
is not just to score the grammaticality of sentences

1129
00:50:12,120 --> 00:50:13,887
in English, but actually that they're

1130
00:50:13,887 --> 00:50:15,720
going to be given instructions, that they're

1131
00:50:15,720 --> 00:50:17,340
going to be given in-context examples,

1132
00:50:17,340 --> 00:50:19,215
that they're going to be asked to reason step

1133
00:50:19,215 --> 00:50:20,990
by step when they're in use?

1134
00:50:20,990 --> 00:50:21,670
Question first.

1135
00:50:21,670 --> 00:50:24,958
AUDIENCE: My question is, does it make sense to say that you

1136
00:50:24,958 --> 00:50:27,250
don't need to fine-tune any more just because it's good

1137
00:50:27,250 --> 00:50:29,670
at everything, or there's, like, better--

1138
00:50:29,670 --> 00:50:32,130
JACOB ANDREAS: So the question is, does it make sense

1139
00:50:32,130 --> 00:50:33,547
to say you don't need to fine-tune

1140
00:50:33,547 --> 00:50:36,250
any more because the model is good at everything?

1141
00:50:36,250 --> 00:50:38,900
This is something where it depends a lot on the task

1142
00:50:38,900 --> 00:50:40,150
that you're trying to perform.

1143
00:50:40,150 --> 00:50:43,890
Certainly, if you have some totally novel thing

1144
00:50:43,890 --> 00:50:48,850
or some weird looking input, you can get models

1145
00:50:48,850 --> 00:50:51,290
into a better place by fine-tuning them

1146
00:50:51,290 --> 00:50:53,630
than by providing examples in context

1147
00:50:53,630 --> 00:50:56,290
if you have lots and lots and lots of examples.

1148
00:50:56,290 --> 00:50:59,425
I think right now, the balance of evidence is in favor

1149
00:50:59,425 --> 00:51:02,050
of, you'd still rather do some kind of fine-tuning on them than

1150
00:51:02,050 --> 00:51:04,828
just dump 100,000 examples in context.

1151
00:51:04,828 --> 00:51:06,370
That being said, this is also an area

1152
00:51:06,370 --> 00:51:09,330
that's moving really fast as the context over which

1153
00:51:09,330 --> 00:51:12,250
these models can operate gets longer and longer

1154
00:51:12,250 --> 00:51:17,700
and is something that could change.

1155
00:51:17,700 --> 00:51:21,700
But most people don't have the ability

1156
00:51:21,700 --> 00:51:26,380
to fine-tune a very large model in the first place

1157
00:51:26,380 --> 00:51:29,520
or can only do that once and not a gajillion times.

1158
00:51:29,520 --> 00:51:31,520
And so just from a practical perspective,

1159
00:51:31,520 --> 00:51:33,540
often, this is kind of the best tool

1160
00:51:33,540 --> 00:51:36,260
that we have for steering these models.

1161
00:51:36,260 --> 00:51:38,393
But then that feeds into the next question of,

1162
00:51:38,393 --> 00:51:40,060
given that this is how people are mostly

1163
00:51:40,060 --> 00:51:41,640
going to be interacting with them,

1164
00:51:41,640 --> 00:51:43,098
what should we be doing differently

1165
00:51:43,098 --> 00:51:45,335
when we train the models so that they will do better

1166
00:51:45,335 --> 00:51:47,460
at in-context learning, so that they will do better

1167
00:51:47,460 --> 00:51:49,980
at instruction following, so that they will do better

1168
00:51:49,980 --> 00:51:51,240
at chain-of-thought reasoning?

1169
00:51:56,220 --> 00:51:57,120
Sorry.

1170
00:51:57,120 --> 00:52:00,660
Skipping ahead here.

1171
00:52:00,660 --> 00:52:04,900
So we set up before that one simple and effective trick

1172
00:52:04,900 --> 00:52:07,980
that you can do, even before any of this chain-of-thought stuff,

1173
00:52:07,980 --> 00:52:13,060
was just to give models these x, y examples in context.

1174
00:52:13,060 --> 00:52:15,670
We saw also that you could give natural language

1175
00:52:15,670 --> 00:52:21,530
descriptions of tasks and ask models to perform these tasks.

1176
00:52:21,530 --> 00:52:30,670
But here, if you, for example--

1177
00:52:30,670 --> 00:52:31,510
sorry.

1178
00:52:31,510 --> 00:52:33,430
To step through this piece by piece.

1179
00:52:33,430 --> 00:52:35,930
If you just give a model the string, "This quiet,

1180
00:52:35,930 --> 00:52:41,590
introspective and entertaining independent is worth thinking,"

1181
00:52:41,590 --> 00:52:44,550
it's not even clear what the task is going to be.

1182
00:52:44,550 --> 00:52:48,873
And so models, by default, when you give them an individual x,

1183
00:52:48,873 --> 00:52:50,290
they're not going to give you a y,

1184
00:52:50,290 --> 00:52:51,710
they're just going to give you some other text that

1185
00:52:51,710 --> 00:52:52,650
looks of related.

1186
00:52:52,650 --> 00:52:59,470
So here was some, I think, GPT-3 or 2, late 2 model

1187
00:52:59,470 --> 00:53:00,730
given this sentence as input.

1188
00:53:00,730 --> 00:53:03,022
And what you get out is not a sentiment label for this,

1189
00:53:03,022 --> 00:53:05,910
but just more text kind of in the same vein.

1190
00:53:05,910 --> 00:53:08,550
If you just modify the format of this a little bit,

1191
00:53:08,550 --> 00:53:10,410
and you prefix this thing with review,

1192
00:53:10,410 --> 00:53:13,480
and then you put answer at the end, even here,

1193
00:53:13,480 --> 00:53:16,840
the model doesn't know the answer to what question.

1194
00:53:16,840 --> 00:53:19,640
You get, "This thought-provoking independent film is worth

1195
00:53:19,640 --> 00:53:20,180
a watch."

1196
00:53:20,180 --> 00:53:21,930
Maybe the model is inferred here that it's

1197
00:53:21,930 --> 00:53:24,880
supposed to be doing grammar correction task rather

1198
00:53:24,880 --> 00:53:27,360
than a sentiment analysis task.

1199
00:53:27,360 --> 00:53:29,972
If you give a detailed natural language instruction, now,

1200
00:53:29,972 --> 00:53:31,680
there's, in principle, enough information

1201
00:53:31,680 --> 00:53:34,210
in the input for the model to figure out

1202
00:53:34,210 --> 00:53:35,960
what it's supposed to be doing and produce

1203
00:53:35,960 --> 00:53:37,660
a positive word as output.

1204
00:53:37,660 --> 00:53:42,320
But even this behavior is quite brittle in models

1205
00:53:42,320 --> 00:53:44,360
that are off the shelf.

1206
00:53:44,360 --> 00:53:47,800
And one reason for that is that there

1207
00:53:47,800 --> 00:53:49,400
are lots of-- again, thinking of these

1208
00:53:49,400 --> 00:53:51,543
as like models of random documents on the internet,

1209
00:53:51,543 --> 00:53:52,960
there are lots of ways of starting

1210
00:53:52,960 --> 00:53:54,460
a document with something that looks

1211
00:53:54,460 --> 00:53:56,460
like an instruction and then an input

1212
00:53:56,460 --> 00:53:59,955
and continuing that document without actually providing

1213
00:53:59,955 --> 00:54:01,580
the text that follows that instruction.

1214
00:54:01,580 --> 00:54:03,400
So here, we've prompted the model with,

1215
00:54:03,400 --> 00:54:04,900
"Answer the following question.

1216
00:54:04,900 --> 00:54:07,880
Why isn't the next-word prediction objective enough

1217
00:54:07,880 --> 00:54:10,650
to enable generic instruction-following?"

1218
00:54:10,650 --> 00:54:13,310
The model, rather than producing an answer here,

1219
00:54:13,310 --> 00:54:16,328
starts to generate more questions of the same kind,

1220
00:54:16,328 --> 00:54:18,370
maybe assuming that, then, all of those questions

1221
00:54:18,370 --> 00:54:23,570
will be handed off to a reader to answer or something.

1222
00:54:23,570 --> 00:54:28,930
And so one thing that has become part of the pretraining process

1223
00:54:28,930 --> 00:54:30,410
after you've gone out and learned

1224
00:54:30,410 --> 00:54:33,130
to do next token prediction on the entire input

1225
00:54:33,130 --> 00:54:34,570
is to realize, OK.

1226
00:54:34,570 --> 00:54:37,690
99% of what people are going to be using these models for

1227
00:54:37,690 --> 00:54:39,810
is instruction following tasks, so let's

1228
00:54:39,810 --> 00:54:42,930
actually just give them targeted supervision on instruction

1229
00:54:42,930 --> 00:54:43,630
following tasks.

1230
00:54:43,630 --> 00:54:46,837
And so if you interact with any modern language model now,

1231
00:54:46,837 --> 00:54:48,670
and you say, "Answer the following question.

1232
00:54:48,670 --> 00:54:51,890
Why isn't the next-word prediction an objective enough?"

1233
00:54:51,890 --> 00:54:54,850
It will answer the question.

1234
00:54:54,850 --> 00:54:56,267
And this is not a natural behavior

1235
00:54:56,267 --> 00:54:58,267
that you get just from training on the internet.

1236
00:54:58,267 --> 00:55:01,010
You actually do need to do some amount of focused fine-tuning

1237
00:55:01,010 --> 00:55:03,970
to get this kind of zero-shot instruction following behavior.

1238
00:55:03,970 --> 00:55:05,670
And in a previous generation of models,

1239
00:55:05,670 --> 00:55:07,128
if you wanted to do this, you would

1240
00:55:07,128 --> 00:55:10,940
have had to give lots and lots more examples of questions

1241
00:55:10,940 --> 00:55:16,220
or really careful formatting of the output and so on.

1242
00:55:16,220 --> 00:55:19,320
And so the main technical thing here,

1243
00:55:19,320 --> 00:55:22,120
which is not actually all that technically interesting at all,

1244
00:55:22,120 --> 00:55:25,200
is just to go out and gather a bunch of targeted instruction

1245
00:55:25,200 --> 00:55:28,420
following data in a way that will cause models to,

1246
00:55:28,420 --> 00:55:31,180
by default, assume an instruction following

1247
00:55:31,180 --> 00:55:33,100
interpretation of inputs rather than

1248
00:55:33,100 --> 00:55:36,140
other kinds of interpretations.

1249
00:55:36,140 --> 00:55:38,380
And there are a couple of different schemes

1250
00:55:38,380 --> 00:55:41,140
that people proposed for following

1251
00:55:41,140 --> 00:55:43,420
this in the literature.

1252
00:55:43,420 --> 00:55:46,540
The first thing which happened mostly in the academic community

1253
00:55:46,540 --> 00:55:47,860
was just to say, OK.

1254
00:55:47,860 --> 00:55:50,820
Let's take all of our existing standard NLP data

1255
00:55:50,820 --> 00:55:54,200
sets for sentiment analysis, for machine translation,

1256
00:55:54,200 --> 00:55:57,780
for text summarization, for sentence classification

1257
00:55:57,780 --> 00:56:01,420
problems of other kinds, and just fine-tune language

1258
00:56:01,420 --> 00:56:04,140
models on all of these things all at the same time

1259
00:56:04,140 --> 00:56:07,870
with little templates that had natural language

1260
00:56:07,870 --> 00:56:11,670
descriptions of each tasks and examples of inputs and outputs.

1261
00:56:11,670 --> 00:56:13,550
And this actually worked pretty well.

1262
00:56:13,550 --> 00:56:17,150
And even on small scale academic-sized models,

1263
00:56:17,150 --> 00:56:19,710
just essentially synthetically generating

1264
00:56:19,710 --> 00:56:23,310
a bunch of instructions derived from standard data

1265
00:56:23,310 --> 00:56:26,950
sets and standard tasks that people had put together

1266
00:56:26,950 --> 00:56:28,870
got you a reasonably long way.

1267
00:56:28,870 --> 00:56:32,230
But it turns out, if you really want high quality, totally

1268
00:56:32,230 --> 00:56:35,550
general instruction following behavior like this and question

1269
00:56:35,550 --> 00:56:37,670
answering behavior like this, there's really

1270
00:56:37,670 --> 00:56:40,710
no substitute for just going out and collecting

1271
00:56:40,710 --> 00:56:44,170
a bunch of new data yourself for as many tasks

1272
00:56:44,170 --> 00:56:46,610
as you can imagine, as many inputs as you can imagine.

1273
00:56:46,610 --> 00:56:50,630
And so a lot of what goes on or is believed

1274
00:56:50,630 --> 00:56:55,350
to go on inside the big LLM company shops right now

1275
00:56:55,350 --> 00:56:57,710
is exactly this, just various clever ways

1276
00:56:57,710 --> 00:57:02,630
of collecting diverse, comprehensive kinds

1277
00:57:02,630 --> 00:57:04,400
of instructions that users are eventually

1278
00:57:04,400 --> 00:57:06,120
going to ask their models so that they

1279
00:57:06,120 --> 00:57:10,320
can fine-tune on that data.

1280
00:57:10,320 --> 00:57:11,040
Yeah.

1281
00:57:11,040 --> 00:57:11,707
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

1282
00:57:11,707 --> 00:57:13,900
This was a couple topics back.

1283
00:57:13,900 --> 00:57:17,360
But when you showed that just adding step by step

1284
00:57:17,360 --> 00:57:22,560
increased the accuracy, is that just for that same word

1285
00:57:22,560 --> 00:57:25,780
problem plus step by step as the entire input,

1286
00:57:25,780 --> 00:57:28,480
but that accuracy doesn't transfer

1287
00:57:28,480 --> 00:57:34,720
to if you just ask the same prompt, and what's the answer?

1288
00:57:34,720 --> 00:57:36,640
It's able to answer that math question

1289
00:57:36,640 --> 00:57:41,440
when you add that input prompt, but it still wasn't retrained

1290
00:57:41,440 --> 00:57:45,200
or learned anything to answer the question correctly

1291
00:57:45,200 --> 00:57:48,043
with the prompt that is not telling it to go [INAUDIBLE].

1292
00:57:48,043 --> 00:57:49,460
JACOB ANDREAS: Yeah, that's right.

1293
00:57:49,460 --> 00:57:52,140
So for all-- let me see if I can find this specific table.

1294
00:57:52,140 --> 00:57:55,200
But for all the examples that we were looking at here,

1295
00:57:55,200 --> 00:57:57,880
in none of these cases are we--

1296
00:57:57,880 --> 00:57:59,880
I guess now, we're a little out of order,

1297
00:57:59,880 --> 00:58:02,090
because these are models that have been instruction

1298
00:58:02,090 --> 00:58:04,830
tuned following the procedure that I showed you later on.

1299
00:58:04,830 --> 00:58:08,670
But it's the same base model in all of these cases.

1300
00:58:08,670 --> 00:58:10,610
And all that's changed is basically

1301
00:58:10,610 --> 00:58:15,210
the text that goes in front of the answer.

1302
00:58:15,210 --> 00:58:16,928
Yeah.

1303
00:58:16,928 --> 00:58:18,470
AUDIENCE: Maybe jumping the gun here,

1304
00:58:18,470 --> 00:58:21,037
but you said collect large data sets from users.

1305
00:58:21,037 --> 00:58:21,870
JACOB ANDREAS: Yeah.

1306
00:58:21,870 --> 00:58:26,930
AUDIENCE: Why not have a system synthetically created

1307
00:58:26,930 --> 00:58:28,733
to augment data sets for instruction?

1308
00:58:28,733 --> 00:58:30,150
JACOB ANDREAS: So the question is,

1309
00:58:30,150 --> 00:58:31,410
why not have the system synthetically

1310
00:58:31,410 --> 00:58:32,350
create these things?

1311
00:58:32,350 --> 00:58:35,630
Well, so there's two things that you could do synthetically.

1312
00:58:35,630 --> 00:58:38,130
You could just ask models to generate,

1313
00:58:38,130 --> 00:58:41,250
what are kinds of instructions that users

1314
00:58:41,250 --> 00:58:43,610
might issue in the future?

1315
00:58:43,610 --> 00:58:47,837
And maybe if you have a lot of instructions of the kind

1316
00:58:47,837 --> 00:58:50,170
that you want to be able to use eventually, and you say,

1317
00:58:50,170 --> 00:58:51,870
generate more stuff in this genre,

1318
00:58:51,870 --> 00:58:54,312
you will get some interesting generalization there.

1319
00:58:54,312 --> 00:58:55,770
And then you can take those things,

1320
00:58:55,770 --> 00:58:58,810
and you can go out and ask people to label them.

1321
00:58:58,810 --> 00:59:02,560
And that is certainly part of the process everywhere.

1322
00:59:02,560 --> 00:59:04,060
Another thing that you could imagine

1323
00:59:04,060 --> 00:59:07,692
doing with synthetic data is asking the model

1324
00:59:07,692 --> 00:59:09,900
both to generate a bunch of instructions and the text

1325
00:59:09,900 --> 00:59:14,187
that follows those instructions and then fine-tune on that.

1326
00:59:14,187 --> 00:59:16,520
And this is something that we can talk more about later.

1327
00:59:16,520 --> 00:59:19,120
But if you just do that, if you just say, generate some data

1328
00:59:19,120 --> 00:59:21,340
and then fine-tune on that same data,

1329
00:59:21,340 --> 00:59:26,660
the model can't possibly have learned to do anything new.

1330
00:59:26,660 --> 00:59:29,140
Information, theoretically, can't get something

1331
00:59:29,140 --> 00:59:30,660
from nothing.

1332
00:59:30,660 --> 00:59:33,460
And so when you're in-- if you want

1333
00:59:33,460 --> 00:59:35,380
to train a model on synthetic data,

1334
00:59:35,380 --> 00:59:38,758
you either need some sort of outside source of information

1335
00:59:38,758 --> 00:59:40,300
that's going to filter that data down

1336
00:59:40,300 --> 00:59:46,720
to the good stuff or some difference between the process

1337
00:59:46,720 --> 00:59:48,220
that's generating the synthetic data

1338
00:59:48,220 --> 00:59:50,470
and the process that's training on the synthetic data.

1339
00:59:50,470 --> 00:59:53,460
And so one concrete example you can imagine

1340
00:59:53,460 --> 00:59:55,280
doing for this instruction following thing,

1341
00:59:55,280 --> 00:59:59,730
which I think people have tried, is to say,

1342
00:59:59,730 --> 01:00:02,910
I'm going to write a really good prompt that really makes

1343
01:00:02,910 --> 01:00:05,430
it very clear that what the model is supposed to be doing

1344
01:00:05,430 --> 01:00:07,472
is instruction following and not any other thing.

1345
01:00:07,472 --> 01:00:09,990
I'm going to have a ton of few-shot examples,

1346
01:00:09,990 --> 01:00:16,690
and I'm going to have very explicit meta

1347
01:00:16,690 --> 01:00:18,190
instructions saying, you're supposed

1348
01:00:18,190 --> 01:00:19,963
to be an instruction following model.

1349
01:00:19,963 --> 01:00:22,130
I'm going to sample a bunch of data from that model,

1350
01:00:22,130 --> 01:00:23,610
and then I'm going to fine-tune on that data.

1351
01:00:23,610 --> 01:00:25,360
And what you get there is not a model that

1352
01:00:25,360 --> 01:00:27,510
knows how to do anything new, but just a model that

1353
01:00:27,510 --> 01:00:29,950
behaves like that really good prompt that you wrote

1354
01:00:29,950 --> 01:00:32,010
is sitting there in the context all the time

1355
01:00:32,010 --> 01:00:36,150
so that the user doesn't have to type it out themselves.

1356
01:00:36,150 --> 01:00:37,930
That trick is called context distillation,

1357
01:00:37,930 --> 01:00:39,597
and it's like one of the standard things

1358
01:00:39,597 --> 01:00:42,170
that you can do with synthetic data that works.

1359
01:00:42,170 --> 01:00:45,770
But it really just moves mass around in the output space,

1360
01:00:45,770 --> 01:00:48,130
and it doesn't tell the model how to do anything new.

1361
01:00:53,470 --> 01:00:54,970
We can do more questions at the end.

1362
01:00:54,970 --> 01:00:59,900
I do want to get ahead to the last bit of this.

1363
01:00:59,900 --> 01:01:06,480
And so coming back to things that change

1364
01:01:06,480 --> 01:01:08,360
at scale or capabilities that emerge

1365
01:01:08,360 --> 01:01:13,800
as models get bigger and bigger, instruction tuning of the kind

1366
01:01:13,800 --> 01:01:16,720
that we were describing, like doing some focused fine-tuning

1367
01:01:16,720 --> 01:01:19,480
on targeted instruction data, does actually

1368
01:01:19,480 --> 01:01:21,880
seem to be quite important for getting

1369
01:01:21,880 --> 01:01:24,760
at some of these capabilities.

1370
01:01:24,760 --> 01:01:27,600
And so looking especially at the second plot

1371
01:01:27,600 --> 01:01:31,360
here, if you take a model, you just do larger and larger scale

1372
01:01:31,360 --> 01:01:33,620
pretraining without this instruction tuning phase,

1373
01:01:33,620 --> 01:01:36,520
it gets better at some standard collection of language

1374
01:01:36,520 --> 01:01:40,000
understanding tasks, but not at a particularly impressive rate.

1375
01:01:40,000 --> 01:01:42,600
If you grow the model, and in parallel, you

1376
01:01:42,600 --> 01:01:48,560
do this instruction tuning step at the end,

1377
01:01:48,560 --> 01:01:51,560
then you start to see really much greater improvements

1378
01:01:51,560 --> 01:01:52,060
with scale.

1379
01:01:52,060 --> 01:01:54,810
So these things interact with each other in a positive way.

1380
01:01:54,810 --> 01:01:56,970
You need targeted training data, and you

1381
01:01:56,970 --> 01:01:58,530
need really large models in order

1382
01:01:58,530 --> 01:02:00,850
to get good general purpose instruction following

1383
01:02:00,850 --> 01:02:04,050
capabilities.

1384
01:02:04,050 --> 01:02:06,693
And is this all we need?

1385
01:02:06,693 --> 01:02:08,610
Or is this the best thing that we can possibly

1386
01:02:08,610 --> 01:02:10,570
do, knowing that we're going to live in a world

1387
01:02:10,570 --> 01:02:15,490
where people are mostly interacting with these models

1388
01:02:15,490 --> 01:02:17,810
by issuing instructions and asking them to do some

1389
01:02:17,810 --> 01:02:20,290
of chain-of-thought reasoning?

1390
01:02:20,290 --> 01:02:22,490
Consider the following set of instructions

1391
01:02:22,490 --> 01:02:26,110
that a user might issue to one of these models.

1392
01:02:28,770 --> 01:02:30,130
And these are all--

1393
01:02:33,170 --> 01:02:34,760
sorry.

1394
01:02:34,760 --> 01:02:36,510
Coming back to what we were saying before,

1395
01:02:36,510 --> 01:02:39,890
the data collection process for instruction tuning

1396
01:02:39,890 --> 01:02:41,110
looks something like this.

1397
01:02:41,110 --> 01:02:43,110
We come up with a set of instruction prompts.

1398
01:02:43,110 --> 01:02:46,422
We ask human annotators to label them,

1399
01:02:46,422 --> 01:02:48,130
and then we just maximize the probability

1400
01:02:48,130 --> 01:02:53,220
of these responses given the instructions that preceded them.

1401
01:02:53,220 --> 01:02:59,220
But you're going to run into some issues here.

1402
01:02:59,220 --> 01:03:03,260
One of them, maybe, is that the data

1403
01:03:03,260 --> 01:03:05,940
is going to change after you train the model,

1404
01:03:05,940 --> 01:03:08,220
so maybe people weren't knowledgeable enough

1405
01:03:08,220 --> 01:03:11,860
to say, by default, when somebody is asking

1406
01:03:11,860 --> 01:03:14,420
a transformer, when they're using transformer with a capital

1407
01:03:14,420 --> 01:03:17,140
T, they mean the machine learning model and not

1408
01:03:17,140 --> 01:03:18,480
the electrical component.

1409
01:03:18,480 --> 01:03:22,560
Maybe in 2017, the data that we were collecting,

1410
01:03:22,560 --> 01:03:24,660
nobody was asking for explanations of transformers

1411
01:03:24,660 --> 01:03:29,140
because nobody knew what a transformer was.

1412
01:03:29,140 --> 01:03:34,660
Similarly, if in this question asking data, if when you go out

1413
01:03:34,660 --> 01:03:37,260
and you ask your users to issue--

1414
01:03:37,260 --> 01:03:39,955
or you collect a bunch of instructions and then you get

1415
01:03:39,955 --> 01:03:42,580
people to label those, and you always label them with the right

1416
01:03:42,580 --> 01:03:48,140
answer, then in the future, if somebody asks an impossible

1417
01:03:48,140 --> 01:03:52,110
question to the language model, it has never anywhere in its

1418
01:03:52,110 --> 01:03:54,390
training data, or its fine-tuning data, at least,

1419
01:03:54,390 --> 01:03:56,110
seen somebody say, "I don't know."

1420
01:03:56,110 --> 01:03:57,870
And so of all of the possible responses

1421
01:03:57,870 --> 01:04:01,410
you could give to who won the 2028 World Cup,

1422
01:04:01,410 --> 01:04:05,710
maybe the model knows enough about soccer

1423
01:04:05,710 --> 01:04:08,253
to know that it should be the name of a country.

1424
01:04:08,253 --> 01:04:09,670
Maybe it knows enough about soccer

1425
01:04:09,670 --> 01:04:11,930
to know what countries are soccer powerhouses

1426
01:04:11,930 --> 01:04:13,850
and that Argentina is a plausible winner

1427
01:04:13,850 --> 01:04:17,590
and maybe the US men's team is not a plausible winner

1428
01:04:17,590 --> 01:04:21,590
of the 2028 World Cup.

1429
01:04:21,590 --> 01:04:24,390
It doesn't assign high probability to,

1430
01:04:24,390 --> 01:04:27,130
this is an impossible question to answer, because it's never,

1431
01:04:27,130 --> 01:04:29,310
in the training data, seen a question that

1432
01:04:29,310 --> 01:04:30,690
was impossible to answer.

1433
01:04:30,690 --> 01:04:33,830
And more generally, even if it saw a impossible questions

1434
01:04:33,830 --> 01:04:36,390
to answer, the language model doesn't

1435
01:04:36,390 --> 01:04:38,990
know what the year is, so it doesn't necessarily

1436
01:04:38,990 --> 01:04:42,350
know-- by default, unless it's been fine-tuned to output 2024

1437
01:04:42,350 --> 01:04:44,750
when somebody asks what the year is.

1438
01:04:44,750 --> 01:04:46,550
And so it doesn't know what things

1439
01:04:46,550 --> 01:04:49,440
are in principle answerable and what things are not

1440
01:04:49,440 --> 01:04:50,920
even in principle answerable.

1441
01:04:50,920 --> 01:04:53,860
And so what you'd really like in these cases is for the model

1442
01:04:53,860 --> 01:04:55,360
to say something like, I don't know.

1443
01:04:55,360 --> 01:04:58,382
This is not part of my training data.

1444
01:04:58,382 --> 01:05:00,840
But you have the chicken and egg problem because your human

1445
01:05:00,840 --> 01:05:02,960
annotators aren't going to know what the model doesn't know

1446
01:05:02,960 --> 01:05:05,560
and therefore can't supervise it to say, "I don't know,"

1447
01:05:05,560 --> 01:05:07,960
in appropriate places.

1448
01:05:07,960 --> 01:05:11,383
Similarly, as a model developer, there

1449
01:05:11,383 --> 01:05:12,800
might be certain kinds of requests

1450
01:05:12,800 --> 01:05:14,560
that we don't want models to answer

1451
01:05:14,560 --> 01:05:16,720
at all in the first place.

1452
01:05:16,720 --> 01:05:20,280
It's a bad PR look for OpenAI or Google

1453
01:05:20,280 --> 01:05:22,600
or Microsoft if you can ask, give me

1454
01:05:22,600 --> 01:05:24,180
ideas on how to cheat on my midterm,

1455
01:05:24,180 --> 01:05:26,222
and the model comes up with lots of ideas for how

1456
01:05:26,222 --> 01:05:27,720
to cheat on the midterm.

1457
01:05:27,720 --> 01:05:30,077
Really, for many users, what they want,

1458
01:05:30,077 --> 01:05:32,160
if they're putting a language model out-- or many,

1459
01:05:32,160 --> 01:05:34,800
I guess, model builders, if they're putting a language model

1460
01:05:34,800 --> 01:05:37,860
out into the world, what they want is a model that will say,

1461
01:05:37,860 --> 01:05:40,280
I don't know-- or sorry, I can't help you in response

1462
01:05:40,280 --> 01:05:44,420
to some category of requests.

1463
01:05:44,420 --> 01:05:47,310
In fact, if you go to ChatGPT, and you say,

1464
01:05:47,310 --> 01:05:49,420
give me ideas for how to cheat on the midterm,

1465
01:05:49,420 --> 01:05:51,170
it gives you a bunch of ideas that are not

1466
01:05:51,170 --> 01:05:52,590
about how to cheat on the midterm,

1467
01:05:52,590 --> 01:05:56,770
but instead a list of activities that you

1468
01:05:56,770 --> 01:05:59,570
could use to get better.

1469
01:05:59,570 --> 01:06:04,090
And it gives you a little preachy, this is bad for you.

1470
01:06:04,090 --> 01:06:08,930
Another funny thing that has been observed in--

1471
01:06:08,930 --> 01:06:12,430
if you just do instruction tuning and nothing else--

1472
01:06:12,430 --> 01:06:15,570
is that certain categories of questions,

1473
01:06:15,570 --> 01:06:19,690
models actually get worse at answering, or at least

1474
01:06:19,690 --> 01:06:22,490
become more predisposed to produce incorrect answers

1475
01:06:22,490 --> 01:06:23,750
as they get larger and larger.

1476
01:06:23,750 --> 01:06:26,450
So here's an example from this truthful QA data

1477
01:06:26,450 --> 01:06:29,410
set where you're asking models of various sizes,

1478
01:06:29,410 --> 01:06:31,070
what happens if you smash a mirror?

1479
01:06:31,070 --> 01:06:32,870
If you ask a 300 million parameter model,

1480
01:06:32,870 --> 01:06:34,328
what happens if you smash a mirror?

1481
01:06:34,328 --> 01:06:35,930
It says, you smash a mirror.

1482
01:06:35,930 --> 01:06:36,710
Not wrong.

1483
01:06:36,710 --> 01:06:38,850
Reasonably high probability.

1484
01:06:38,850 --> 01:06:41,180
You ask a 13 billion instruction tuned model, what

1485
01:06:41,180 --> 01:06:42,430
happens if you smash a mirror?

1486
01:06:42,430 --> 01:06:44,580
It defines a mirror for you.

1487
01:06:44,580 --> 01:06:49,340
You ask a 7 billion instruction tuned model.

1488
01:06:49,340 --> 01:06:51,317
It says it'll shatter into a million pieces.

1489
01:06:51,317 --> 01:06:52,900
And you ask a really big model, and it

1490
01:06:52,900 --> 01:06:55,940
says you'll have seven years of bad luck.

1491
01:06:55,940 --> 01:06:58,380
And the reason or the way to think about what's

1492
01:06:58,380 --> 01:07:00,447
happening here is that the model,

1493
01:07:00,447 --> 01:07:02,780
as it gets bigger and bigger, is not just getting better

1494
01:07:02,780 --> 01:07:05,020
at instruction following, but it's

1495
01:07:05,020 --> 01:07:10,260
modeling, in a finer grained way, the training distribution.

1496
01:07:10,260 --> 01:07:12,558
And I think this is probably still true.

1497
01:07:12,558 --> 01:07:14,100
Certainly, it was true at some point.

1498
01:07:14,100 --> 01:07:16,392
If you looked at what text was likely to co-occur with,

1499
01:07:16,392 --> 01:07:18,640
what happens if you smash a mirror on the internet?

1500
01:07:18,640 --> 01:07:25,180
It's mostly stuff talking about the superstition and not

1501
01:07:25,180 --> 01:07:26,722
about the physics of mirror smashing.

1502
01:07:26,722 --> 01:07:29,263
And so as you get better and better at modeling that training

1503
01:07:29,263 --> 01:07:31,980
distribution, if you don't have some way of explicitly signaling

1504
01:07:31,980 --> 01:07:34,960
to the model that it's always supposed to answer truthfully,

1505
01:07:34,960 --> 01:07:37,333
then it becomes able to distinguish between,

1506
01:07:37,333 --> 01:07:39,500
this is the kind of question where, on the internet,

1507
01:07:39,500 --> 01:07:41,327
people usually give a truthful answer,

1508
01:07:41,327 --> 01:07:43,660
and this is the kind of question where, on the internet,

1509
01:07:43,660 --> 01:07:45,920
people usually give a superstitious answer.

1510
01:07:45,920 --> 01:07:47,480
And so this Lin et al.

1511
01:07:47,480 --> 01:07:51,100
Paper documented a whole category of phenomena

1512
01:07:51,100 --> 01:07:53,480
like this where models actually, by default,

1513
01:07:53,480 --> 01:07:56,780
get worse as they get bigger.

1514
01:07:56,780 --> 01:07:57,620
OK.

1515
01:07:57,620 --> 01:08:02,000
So just to say these two things again, one more time.

1516
01:08:02,000 --> 01:08:03,500
We want models to say, I don't know.

1517
01:08:03,500 --> 01:08:06,620
Sometimes, we want models to answer in accordance

1518
01:08:06,620 --> 01:08:08,580
with what they actually know and what they

1519
01:08:08,580 --> 01:08:10,120
think the user wants to hear.

1520
01:08:10,120 --> 01:08:13,540
Sometimes, we often want them to not give answers,

1521
01:08:13,540 --> 01:08:18,620
even when they know the answers, for user safety reasons

1522
01:08:18,620 --> 01:08:20,300
or whatever.

1523
01:08:20,300 --> 01:08:22,120
And fundamentally, in all of these cases,

1524
01:08:22,120 --> 01:08:25,580
it's hard to get models to output the right answers just

1525
01:08:25,580 --> 01:08:27,779
by providing targeted supervision because we

1526
01:08:27,779 --> 01:08:30,420
don't have access to their internal knowledge.

1527
01:08:30,420 --> 01:08:35,020
We don't know what they and therefore can't surgically

1528
01:08:35,020 --> 01:08:36,859
intervene on exactly the questions where

1529
01:08:36,859 --> 01:08:38,526
we would like them to say, I don't know,

1530
01:08:38,526 --> 01:08:41,270
or any of these other things.

1531
01:08:41,270 --> 01:08:44,830
And so a standard thing-- and we're really only going to talk

1532
01:08:44,830 --> 01:08:48,270
about this for a little while in the interest of time--

1533
01:08:48,270 --> 01:08:52,550
but is that there's now a third step in this language model

1534
01:08:52,550 --> 01:08:57,029
training pipeline, which is reinforcement learning

1535
01:08:57,029 --> 01:09:01,870
from human feedback phase.

1536
01:09:01,870 --> 01:09:03,950
Here's an example of ChatGPT talking

1537
01:09:03,950 --> 01:09:05,783
like a pirate while describing reinforcement

1538
01:09:05,783 --> 01:09:06,742
learning with feedback.

1539
01:09:06,742 --> 01:09:08,729
I don't know why we included that in the talk,

1540
01:09:08,729 --> 01:09:09,630
but it's there.

1541
01:09:09,630 --> 01:09:10,130
OK.

1542
01:09:10,130 --> 01:09:13,310
So fundamentally, how are we going

1543
01:09:13,310 --> 01:09:16,990
to get around all of the issues that we talked about

1544
01:09:16,990 --> 01:09:19,670
before, this thing where we don't know what the model knows

1545
01:09:19,670 --> 01:09:22,350
and want to control its output in cases where we don't actually

1546
01:09:22,350 --> 01:09:26,550
know exactly what it is supposed to say?

1547
01:09:26,550 --> 01:09:28,807
Consider the following simplified example.

1548
01:09:28,807 --> 01:09:30,390
Suppose now we're just trying to build

1549
01:09:30,390 --> 01:09:33,649
a model to do some specific targeted task,

1550
01:09:33,649 --> 01:09:37,600
say, text summarization, where there's

1551
01:09:37,600 --> 01:09:42,103
many, many good summaries for a model or for any given article.

1552
01:09:42,103 --> 01:09:43,520
Lots of different ways in which we

1553
01:09:43,520 --> 01:09:48,640
could produce a reasonable looking summary

1554
01:09:48,640 --> 01:09:50,720
of some textual article and where

1555
01:09:50,720 --> 01:09:53,439
we want models to learn that they're allowed to output

1556
01:09:53,439 --> 01:09:55,520
a diverse distribution over responses,

1557
01:09:55,520 --> 01:09:57,520
that there's many acceptable things to say here.

1558
01:09:57,520 --> 01:09:59,720
One thing that we could imagine doing

1559
01:09:59,720 --> 01:10:02,360
would be to ask our human annotators

1560
01:10:02,360 --> 01:10:04,800
to generate all of the acceptable summaries and none

1561
01:10:04,800 --> 01:10:07,542
of the unacceptable summaries for a given article,

1562
01:10:07,542 --> 01:10:09,000
write them all down, and then train

1563
01:10:09,000 --> 01:10:12,320
the language model to generate all of them at the same time.

1564
01:10:12,320 --> 01:10:14,680
Obviously, this is not going to be practical

1565
01:10:14,680 --> 01:10:17,940
because that's a huge amount of work for our human annotators,

1566
01:10:17,940 --> 01:10:21,000
and we'd rather not ask them to spend their time that way.

1567
01:10:21,000 --> 01:10:24,080
Another thing-- coming back to the synthetic data question

1568
01:10:24,080 --> 01:10:28,060
from before-- that we could do here is ask the model itself,

1569
01:10:28,060 --> 01:10:31,040
after it's done a little bit of instruction tuning,

1570
01:10:31,040 --> 01:10:32,600
to generate some summaries.

1571
01:10:32,600 --> 01:10:35,480
And here, because it's just a language model,

1572
01:10:35,480 --> 01:10:36,992
we don't have to pay it, or we don't

1573
01:10:36,992 --> 01:10:38,450
have to pay for the electricity, we

1574
01:10:38,450 --> 01:10:42,290
can generate a large, large number of summaries

1575
01:10:42,290 --> 01:10:45,930
without really needing a human in the loop.

1576
01:10:45,930 --> 01:10:47,858
And so the question becomes, how do I

1577
01:10:47,858 --> 01:10:49,650
get from this large collection of summaries

1578
01:10:49,650 --> 01:10:53,210
that I sampled from my model to something

1579
01:10:53,210 --> 01:10:54,970
that will make the model actually better

1580
01:10:54,970 --> 01:10:58,050
at this summarization task?

1581
01:10:58,050 --> 01:11:00,670
Well, if the model is itself the one generating the summaries,

1582
01:11:00,670 --> 01:11:02,712
then in some sense, the only thing that we can do

1583
01:11:02,712 --> 01:11:05,610
is pick out the good summaries or identify the bad summaries,

1584
01:11:05,610 --> 01:11:09,130
and just say, of the summaries that the model itself generated,

1585
01:11:09,130 --> 01:11:11,610
cause it to assign higher probability to the good ones

1586
01:11:11,610 --> 01:11:15,022
and lower probability to the bad ones.

1587
01:11:15,022 --> 01:11:16,730
And so again, one thing that you could do

1588
01:11:16,730 --> 01:11:22,530
is ask your human annotators to go through and not just write

1589
01:11:22,530 --> 01:11:24,970
the summaries themselves, but to label

1590
01:11:24,970 --> 01:11:27,490
the machine-generated summaries either

1591
01:11:27,490 --> 01:11:29,450
by assigning them numerical scores,

1592
01:11:29,450 --> 01:11:31,670
or just giving them thumbs up or thumbs down,

1593
01:11:31,670 --> 01:11:33,900
or expressing pairwise preferences between them,

1594
01:11:33,900 --> 01:11:35,500
or whatever else.

1595
01:11:35,500 --> 01:11:38,938
And this is also a reasonable strategy, but again, something

1596
01:11:38,938 --> 01:11:40,980
that, if we want to do it at a really large scale

1597
01:11:40,980 --> 01:11:42,900
for a large number of summaries, is

1598
01:11:42,900 --> 01:11:47,660
going to require a huge amount of human annotator effort.

1599
01:11:47,660 --> 01:11:51,660
And so what we actually do is something as follows.

1600
01:11:51,660 --> 01:11:55,060
Again, we're working with a normal language model, which

1601
01:11:55,060 --> 01:11:58,640
by analogy to reinforcement learning,

1602
01:11:58,640 --> 01:12:00,780
we're going to briefly refer to as a policy.

1603
01:12:00,780 --> 01:12:02,250
Don't worry about this.

1604
01:12:02,250 --> 01:12:04,500
Again, it's just a thing that takes some text as input

1605
01:12:04,500 --> 01:12:07,460
and generates some text as output.

1606
01:12:07,460 --> 01:12:12,360
Let us suppose for a moment that instead of our human annotators,

1607
01:12:12,360 --> 01:12:16,380
we had some magical black box that could pick up any article

1608
01:12:16,380 --> 01:12:20,300
summary pair and give us some number corresponding to how good

1609
01:12:20,300 --> 01:12:22,940
that summary was.

1610
01:12:22,940 --> 01:12:25,540
Then, all we would really need to do

1611
01:12:25,540 --> 01:12:27,800
is find some way of optimizing this language model,

1612
01:12:27,800 --> 01:12:30,360
choosing the parameters, P, for this language model

1613
01:12:30,360 --> 01:12:34,270
so that the score assigned to the summaries that it generated

1614
01:12:34,270 --> 01:12:38,590
became higher and maybe became as high as possible.

1615
01:12:38,590 --> 01:12:41,668
And given that we only have five minutes left,

1616
01:12:41,668 --> 01:12:44,210
I'm really not going to go too much into the details of this,

1617
01:12:44,210 --> 01:12:48,230
other than to say that in practice, what people do

1618
01:12:48,230 --> 01:12:53,670
is we get our annotators to hand label a bunch of articles

1619
01:12:53,670 --> 01:12:55,910
with thumbs up, thumbs downs, just

1620
01:12:55,910 --> 01:12:57,550
like we were saying over here.

1621
01:12:57,550 --> 01:13:00,230
But we use these not to train the model

1622
01:13:00,230 --> 01:13:03,130
and instead to train a reward model.

1623
01:13:03,130 --> 01:13:05,575
So we train a model not that's generating text,

1624
01:13:05,575 --> 01:13:06,950
but instead a model that's trying

1625
01:13:06,950 --> 01:13:09,070
to reproduce the human annotators' judgments

1626
01:13:09,070 --> 01:13:11,990
about which articles are good and which ones are bad.

1627
01:13:11,990 --> 01:13:14,890
This is an easier task, and this is a really important point.

1628
01:13:14,890 --> 01:13:18,130
This is an easier task than generating articles themselves.

1629
01:13:18,130 --> 01:13:21,510
We've all taken our intro CS theory class.

1630
01:13:21,510 --> 01:13:23,510
Recognition problems generally are easier

1631
01:13:23,510 --> 01:13:25,430
than generation problems.

1632
01:13:25,430 --> 01:13:27,850
And so for summarization, for math, problem solving,

1633
01:13:27,850 --> 01:13:31,600
whatever, it is generally, both computationally

1634
01:13:31,600 --> 01:13:34,520
and from a statistical efficiency standpoint, going

1635
01:13:34,520 --> 01:13:37,760
to be easier to train models to score summaries

1636
01:13:37,760 --> 01:13:38,885
than to generate summaries.

1637
01:13:38,885 --> 01:13:40,510
But as soon as we've done this, as soon

1638
01:13:40,510 --> 01:13:42,600
as we have a good summary score, we're done.

1639
01:13:42,600 --> 01:13:45,280
All we have to do is throw standard optimization

1640
01:13:45,280 --> 01:13:48,160
techniques at this objective right here,

1641
01:13:48,160 --> 01:13:51,160
and what we get out at the end is a model that gets better

1642
01:13:51,160 --> 01:13:53,280
at producing summaries.

1643
01:13:53,280 --> 01:13:56,020
To go just one step further into detail here,

1644
01:13:56,020 --> 01:13:59,720
the standard thing that people do now

1645
01:13:59,720 --> 01:14:02,720
is an algorithm called policy gradient,

1646
01:14:02,720 --> 01:14:05,160
which I think I do not even have written out here.

1647
01:14:05,160 --> 01:14:07,440
But basically, you can turn this objective

1648
01:14:07,440 --> 01:14:10,560
into something that looks like just doing

1649
01:14:10,560 --> 01:14:13,320
normal maximum likelihood estimation where each sample is

1650
01:14:13,320 --> 01:14:16,560
weighted by the score assigned to it by the reward model.

1651
01:14:16,560 --> 01:14:18,320
And so if you've heard of reinforcement

1652
01:14:18,320 --> 01:14:19,840
learning with human feedback, that

1653
01:14:19,840 --> 01:14:21,180
is basically the entire thing.

1654
01:14:21,180 --> 01:14:23,100
You go out, and you train this reward model,

1655
01:14:23,100 --> 01:14:25,280
and then you do RL against the reward model

1656
01:14:25,280 --> 01:14:28,130
that you collected from human judgments.

1657
01:14:28,130 --> 01:14:36,110
Now, going back a couple of slides to here,

1658
01:14:36,110 --> 01:14:38,250
one thing that we said is that--

1659
01:14:38,250 --> 01:14:42,130
or I guess to here.

1660
01:14:42,130 --> 01:14:46,730
One thing that we said is that we would conceptually just like

1661
01:14:46,730 --> 01:14:49,180
it to be the case that the model assigns high probability

1662
01:14:49,180 --> 01:14:50,930
to the things that the people thought were

1663
01:14:50,930 --> 01:14:52,810
good and low probability to the things

1664
01:14:52,810 --> 01:14:54,690
that people thought were bad.

1665
01:14:54,690 --> 01:14:56,890
And there were a bunch of other learning algorithms

1666
01:14:56,890 --> 01:14:59,170
that you can use to do this that don't look like reinforcement

1667
01:14:59,170 --> 01:14:59,670
learning.

1668
01:14:59,670 --> 01:15:02,705
So if you've heard of SLiCK or DPO or any

1669
01:15:02,705 --> 01:15:04,330
of these other things, basically, there

1670
01:15:04,330 --> 01:15:07,690
are other ways of taking these human labels,

1671
01:15:07,690 --> 01:15:10,450
not training a reward model, and just directly optimizing

1672
01:15:10,450 --> 01:15:13,450
your language model to make the good things high probability--

1673
01:15:13,450 --> 01:15:15,770
or I guess the bad things, low probability,

1674
01:15:15,770 --> 01:15:19,610
and the good things, high probability.

1675
01:15:19,610 --> 01:15:20,110
Yeah.

1676
01:15:20,110 --> 01:15:27,020
And that's basically what I wanted to go through today.

1677
01:15:27,020 --> 01:15:29,160
Happy to do some questions at the end.

1678
01:15:29,160 --> 01:15:34,000
But just to pull up one summary slide back from the beginning.

1679
01:15:39,740 --> 01:15:41,100
It's actually this one.

1680
01:15:41,100 --> 01:15:43,660
Really coming back to what Phillip

1681
01:15:43,660 --> 01:15:46,820
was saying at the very beginning of this lecture,

1682
01:15:46,820 --> 01:15:50,677
I think the defining feature of the language model

1683
01:15:50,677 --> 01:15:52,260
era, the defining feature of the world

1684
01:15:52,260 --> 01:15:55,500
that we find ourselves in right now,

1685
01:15:55,500 --> 01:15:58,820
is the stuff that's written in this rightmost column here.

1686
01:15:58,820 --> 01:16:01,560
I think, much to many people's surprise,

1687
01:16:01,560 --> 01:16:05,020
if you do large enough scale training on just tons

1688
01:16:05,020 --> 01:16:06,980
and tons and tons of text data, and then

1689
01:16:06,980 --> 01:16:10,300
a little bit of refinement after the fact that doesn't actually

1690
01:16:10,300 --> 01:16:12,420
change really much about what models

1691
01:16:12,420 --> 01:16:14,940
and mostly just the format in which they produce

1692
01:16:14,940 --> 01:16:16,860
their responses by default and the assumptions

1693
01:16:16,860 --> 01:16:20,220
they make about the inputs that are being given to them,

1694
01:16:20,220 --> 01:16:22,740
you wind up with really flexible, really general purpose

1695
01:16:22,740 --> 01:16:26,230
predictors that can be given instructions,

1696
01:16:26,230 --> 01:16:32,870
that can be given small numbers of examples as input

1697
01:16:32,870 --> 01:16:36,430
and that can then do a relatively broad range of tasks

1698
01:16:36,430 --> 01:16:39,150
without any kind of additional fine-tuning

1699
01:16:39,150 --> 01:16:42,710
or any additional modification of their internal parameters.

1700
01:16:42,710 --> 01:16:47,270
And I think figuring out how far to push this, figuring out

1701
01:16:47,270 --> 01:16:50,830
what comes after this chain-of-thought thing in terms

1702
01:16:50,830 --> 01:16:53,150
of making models better at solving even more

1703
01:16:53,150 --> 01:16:55,870
computationally complex tasks, I think

1704
01:16:55,870 --> 01:17:00,730
are then the research challenges for the post-LLM era.

1705
01:17:00,730 --> 01:17:01,950
And I will wrap up there.

1706
01:17:01,950 --> 01:17:04,730
If folks have questions, I can stick around and answer them.

1707
01:17:04,730 --> 01:17:05,410
But thank you.

1708
01:17:05,410 --> 01:17:07,560
[APPLAUSE]
